// Interview problems: GD&T practice problems written from the notes (Krulikowski, ASME Y14.5-2009). Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_BY=window.EXAMPLES_BY||{}).gdt=[{"id":"1.1","title":"When does a drawing bind the supplier to a standard?","q":"A purchasing engineer sends a supplier a detail drawing that contains feature control frames with position and flatness symbols, but the title block and general notes never mention any dimensioning standard. The supplier asks, \u0027Do I interpret these symbols per ASME Y14.5-2009 or per ISO?\u0027 As the design engineer, what do you say, and what must be changed on the drawing so the question cannot arise again?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A dimensioning and tolerancing standard governs a drawing only when the drawing invokes it; it is not automatic just because symbols appear.\n2. With nothing invoked, the interpretation is ambiguous: ASME Y14.5 and ISO GPS differ in several defaults (e.g. the envelope principle / Rule #1 versus independency, and how some symbols are defined).\n3. Fix: add a note in the title block or general notes, e.g. \u0027DIMENSIONING AND TOLERANCING PER ASME Y14.5-2009\u0027, and state the unit system.\n4. Also state revision/year, since the standard changes between editions.","answers":["The standard applies only if the drawing invokes it","Add a note such as \u0027DIMENSIONING AND TOLERANCING PER ASME Y14.5-2009\u0027 in the title block or general notes"]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Why specify the largest tolerance function allows?","q":"A junior engineer proposes tightening every tolerance on a bracket drawing \u0027to be safe\u0027, arguing that tighter is always better quality and costs only a little more. Explain to them, from the point of view of the whole organization, why this is poor practice, and state what the correct basis for choosing each tolerance is.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Tolerance amount should equal the variation that does not harm function (fit, assembly, performance, safety).\n2. Tolerances drive manufacturing cost (process choice, tooling, fixtures, scrap, machine capability) and inspection cost (gage accuracy, setup, measurement time), so smaller tolerance means more cost.\n3. Unneeded tightness also rejects functionally good parts and can cause disputes between supplier and customer.\n4. Correct approach: functional dimensioning, assign the largest tolerance that still guarantees function, tighten only where analysis or testing demands it.","answers":["Tolerance = largest variation that still preserves function","Tighter tolerances raise manufacturing and inspection cost and scrap good parts"]},{"id":"1.3","title":"Cost of a drawing error found late","q":"A dimension on a drawing is wrong. Compare the consequences of discovering it (a) during drawing review, (b) after tooling has been built and parts produced, and (c) after product has shipped to customers. In an interview you are asked why drawing clarity is stressed so heavily; what is the key point about when errors are caught, and who else is affected besides the designer?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Cost of an error grows at each stage it survives: review (cheap, a revision), after tooling/production (scrap, rework, new tooling, schedule delay), after shipment (recall, warranty, field failures, customer goodwill, possible legal exposure).\n2. A drawing is a legal description of the part; many people (purchasing, suppliers, manufacturing, inspection, quality, assembly, service) interpret it, so a vague or wrong callout multiplies waste across them.\n3. Key point: catch errors as early as possible and write drawings so they have a single meaning.","answers":["Cost grows with each stage the error survives","Many departments and suppliers (up to thousands of people) interpret one drawing, so ambiguity is multiplied"]},{"id":"1.4","title":"Same drawing, ASME shop versus ISO shop","q":"A drawing prepared per ASME Y14.5-2009 for a ⌀20 +0/−0.1 mm ground pin (no form tolerance given) is sent to a European supplier who works to ISO GPS standards by default. Name one important respect in which the default interpretation can differ and what the consequence is for the pin, and state what the drawing should say to avoid the issue.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Under ASME Y14.5 Rule #1 (envelope principle) the limits of size also control the form of an individual regular feature of size: the pin must stay inside a perfect-form envelope at MMC (⌀20) and every local size must be ≥ ⌀19.9.\n2. Under ISO default (independency, ISO 8015) size limits control only local two-point size; form (e.g. bend) is not limited unless separately toleranced, so a bowed pin with every local size in limits can be accepted.\n3. Consequence: a bowed pin could be produced and accepted but fail to assemble.\n4. Fix: invoke the standard by note (ASME Y14.5-2009), or in ISO add the envelope requirement Ⓔ / explicit straightness.","answers":["ASME Rule #1 envelope controls form; ISO default (independency) does not","Invoke the standard on the drawing (ASME Y14.5-2009) or add Ⓔ / form tolerances explicitly"]},{"id":"2.1","title":"Limits and acceptance of an unequal bilateral dimension","q":"A shaft diameter is dimensioned ⌀25.40 +0.05 / −0.02 mm. (a) State the upper and lower limits and the tolerance. (b) Convert it to an equal bilateral tolerance. (c) An inspector measures a local diameter of 25.451 mm and says \u0027that rounds to 25.45, so it passes.\u0027 Is that correct?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Upper limit = 25.40 + 0.05 = 25.45; lower limit = 25.40 − 0.02 = 25.38.\n2. Tolerance = 25.45 − 25.38 = 0.07 mm.\n3. Equal bilateral: mean = (25.45 + 25.38)/2 = 25.415, so ⌀25.415 ±0.035.\n4. Limits are absolute: a stated value is read as followed by infinite zeros, no rounding. 25.451 \u003e 25.45, so the part is out of tolerance and rejected.","answers":["Limits 25.38 and 25.45 mm, tolerance 0.07 mm","Equal bilateral: ⌀25.415 ±0.035","25.451 fails: limits are absolute, no rounding"]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Spot the metric notation errors","q":"A millimeter drawing shows these items: (1) a slot width given as .4 ; (2) a plain length of 56.0 with no tolerance attached; (3) a hole ⌀30.5 with tolerance +0.1 / −0 ; (4) an angle of 0.4° written as .4° . For each, state whether it follows the usual Y14.5 metric conventions and how it should be written.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Values below 1 mm take a leading zero: .4 is wrong, write 0.4.\n2. Metric dimensions omit trailing zeros and the decimal point on whole numbers: 56.0 is wrong, write 56 (a trailing zero is allowed only to match digits of a tolerance).\n3. A nil tolerance is a single 0 with no sign: write +0.1 / 0, not −0.\n4. Angles below 1° also take a leading zero: write 0.4°.\nAll four are incorrect as written.","answers":["(1) 0.4","(2) 56","(3) 30.5 +0.1/0 (nil tolerance has no sign)","(4) 0.4°"]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Tolerance on a basic dimension","q":"A hole is located 40 mm from datum A by a boxed (basic) dimension and its position is controlled by a feature control frame: position ⌀0.30 mm relative to datums A, B, C. The title block says \u0027UNLESS OTHERWISE SPECIFIED, LINEAR ±0.5\u0027. A machinist argues the 40 is therefore 40 ±0.5. Is he right? Where does the location tolerance come from, and what does the basic dimension do in inspection?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A basic dimension is theoretically exact. Title-block (general) tolerances never apply to it.\n2. Its tolerance comes only from the geometric tolerance in the frame (here ⌀0.30 position zone); the 40 locates the center of the tolerance zone from datum A.\n3. In inspection: set the part up on the datum reference frame, build the zone ⌀0.30 centered at exactly 40 from A (and the other basic distances), and check the hole axis lies within it.\n4. So the machinist is wrong; the hole axis may deviate only within a ⌀0.30 zone around the exact position, not ±0.5.","answers":["No: general tolerances do not apply to basic dimensions","Location tolerance = position ⌀0.30 zone centered on the basic location","Basic dimensions locate the zone from the datums"]},{"id":"2.4","title":"Nominal CAD model versus the drawing","q":"A supplier receives a 3D CAD model of a housing whose bore is modeled at exactly ⌀50.000 mm and a drawing that shows no tolerance for the bore, only a title-block note of ±0.5 for \u0027machined dimensions\u0027. The supplier says \u0027the model is the master, so ⌀50.000 is the requirement and ±0.5 is the general rule.\u0027 Discuss what is wrong with relying on the model alone for a precision bore, and what you would specify.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A CAD model holds nominal geometry; real parts vary. \u0027Nominal thinking\u0027 ignores process variation: the model never says how much variation is acceptable.\n2. Dimensions and tolerances on a model/drawing must be explicit, with the datum scheme and tolerance zones, otherwise the default ±0.5 is the only allowance and a ⌀50 ±0.5 bore would not satisfy a precision fit.\n3. Specify a size tolerance for the bore (e.g. ⌀50.00 to 50.03) plus geometric tolerances (position/perpendicularity to datums, form via Rule #1) with datum features identified.\n4. Model and drawing must be consistent and the data set must state which governs.","answers":["Model gives only nominal geometry; tolerance must be stated explicitly","Specify a size tolerance plus geometric tolerances and datums on the bore"]},{"id":"3.1","title":"Square plus-minus zone versus circular zone","q":"A hole is located from two edges by coordinate tolerances of ±0.25 mm in X and ±0.25 mm in Y. (a) What shape and size is the resulting tolerance zone for the hole axis? (b) What is the largest radial distance the axis can be from its nominal position and still pass? (c) If a cylindrical position zone of that same worst-case diameter were used instead, by what percent would the zone area increase compared with the square?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone is a 0.50 × 0.50 mm square centered on the nominal location; area = 0.25 mm².\n2. Worst-case radial deviation is at the corner: 0.25·√2 = 0.354 mm, so a 0.707 mm diameter circle just circumscribes the square.\n3. Circle ⌀0.707: area = π(0.3536)² = 0.393 mm².\n4. Increase = 0.393/0.25 − 1 = 0.571, about 57% more tolerance area (the familiar \u002757% more\u0027 of a cylindrical zone vs the inscribed square).","answers":["0.50 × 0.50 mm square zone","Max radial deviation 0.354 mm (⌀0.707 equivalent)","About 57% larger area"]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Good part rejected by a square zone?","q":"A hole axis is located with coordinate tolerances ±0.15 mm in X and Y. An inspector finds the actual axis offset by 0.14 mm in X and 0.14 mm in Y from nominal. (a) Does it pass the coordinate tolerance? (b) Would it pass if the callout were position ⌀0.30 mm RFS? (c) Would it pass with position ⌀0.42 mm RFS? (d) What does this show about square zones?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Offsets 0.14 ≤ 0.15 in each direction, so it passes the coordinate tolerance.\n2. Radial offset = √(0.14² + 0.14²) = 0.198 mm, i.e. the axis lies at diameter 0.396 mm from the nominal axis.\n3. Position ⌀0.30: needs 0.396 ≤ 0.30, false, so it fails.\n4. Position ⌀0.42: 0.396 ≤ 0.42, passes.\n5. The square zone (⌀0.42 corner-to-corner) accepts parts up to 0.212 radial in diagonal directions but only 0.15 along axes; the zone is non-uniform and direction-dependent, which causes inconsistent acceptance. A cylindrical zone is the same in all directions.","answers":["(a) Pass","(b) Fail: 0.396 \u003e 0.30","(c) Pass: 0.396 ≤ 0.42","Square zones are direction dependent; a circular zone is uniform"]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Chained versus datum-based hole spacing","q":"Four holes in a row are spaced 25 mm apart. Case 1: each hole is dimensioned from the previous hole, 25 ±0.1 mm. Case 2: each hole is dimensioned from a common left edge (datum) as 25, 50, 75 and 100 mm, each ±0.1 mm. In each case, what is the worst-case allowable distance between hole 1 and hole 4, and between hole 1 and hole 2? Which case shows tolerance accumulation, and how does GD\u0026T avoid both problems?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Case 1 (chain): 1 to 4 spans three links, 75 ±0.3 (limits 74.7 to 75.3); 1 to 2 is 25 ±0.1. Tolerance accumulates.\n2. Case 2 (baseline): hole 1 at 25 ±0.1 and hole 4 at 100 ±0.1 from the edge: separation 75 ±0.2 (74.8 to 75.2); 1 to 2 is 25 ±0.2 (24.8 to 25.2) since each hole varies independently from the datum.\n3. Chain accumulates (0.3 over three links); baseline removes accumulation but doubles the pairwise tolerance (±0.2 between neighbors).\n4. GD\u0026T: basic dimensions are exact, so no stack-up; the holes are located with a position tolerance from the datum reference frame, with the tolerance coming only from the frame (e.g. ⌀0.3).","answers":["Chain: 1-4 = 75 ±0.3; 1-2 = 25 ±0.1","Baseline: 1-4 = 75 ±0.2; 1-2 = 25 ±0.2","GD\u0026T uses basic dimensions plus a position frame: no accumulation"]},{"id":"3.4","title":"Are two diameters related by plus-minus sizes?","q":"A drawing shows a ⌀24 ±0.1 mm boss and a ⌀10 ±0.1 mm hole drilled through it, with no datums and no geometric tolerance. The designer says \u0027the hole is obviously concentric with the boss.\u0027 Is the relationship defined by this drawing? What could an inspector with calipers verify, and what would you add to define the intended coaxiality?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Plus-minus size dimensions control only the size of each feature (plus form via Rule #1); they do not relate one feature to another.\n2. Concentricity is not defined: no datum, no zone. Calipers measure only the two sizes; no offset between axes is limited, so any offset passes or fails by opinion.\n3. Add a datum feature symbol to the ⌀24 boss (datum A) and a position frame on the hole, e.g. position ⌀0.2 relative to A (basic 0 location, or runout/position as function demands), or a profile tolerance; the datum reference frame gives the setup for inspection.\n4. Coordinate tolerancing is legitimate for sizes and non-critical features only.","answers":["No: the coaxial relationship is undefined","Add a datum on the boss and a position (or runout) tolerance on the hole referencing it"]},{"id":"4.1","title":"Reading a counterbored hole note","q":"A drawing note on a plate reads: 4X ⌀10.5 ±0.1 THRU, with a counterbore symbol followed by ⌀18 and a depth symbol followed by 6. Describe in words exactly what feature is being called out: how many, the through-hole size, the counterbore diameter and depth, and from which surface the depth is measured. What is the difference between this and a spotface?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. 4X: four identical features.\n2. ⌀10.5 ±0.1 through the full plate thickness (THRU).\n3. Counterbore symbol ⌀18: a cylindrical recess of diameter 18 concentric with each hole, to seat a bolt head (e.g. socket cap screw).\n4. Depth symbol 6: the counterbore is 6 deep, measured from the surface on which the counterbore starts (the top surface) to the flat bottom of the recess.\n5. Spotface: same symbol as counterbore with SF (or a spotface note); it only cleans up the surface to a flat seat, usually a shallow depth, and when no depth is given the minimum to clean up the surface is implied, whereas counterbore depth is specified.","answers":["4 holes ⌀10.5 ±0.1 through; each counterbored ⌀18 to 6 deep from the top surface","Spotface only machines a flat seat (minimum depth to clean up); counterbore has a specified depth"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Why show an overall dimension in parentheses?","q":"A bracket has two stacked steps dimensioned 15 ±0.1 mm and 25 ±0.1 mm. The designer also shows the overall height as (40) in parentheses. (a) What does the parenthesis mean? (b) What range of overall heights actually results from the two toleranced steps? (c) Why would it be a mistake to write 40 ±0.1 for the overall height as well? (d) How do a reference dimension, a basic dimension and a MAX dimension differ in tolerance?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (40) is a reference dimension: information only, derived from other dimensions; it carries no tolerance and is not used for manufacturing or inspection.\n2. Overall height range: 15 + 25 = 40, tolerance ±0.1 ±0.1 = ±0.2, so 39.8 to 40.2 mm.\n3. Toleranced 40 ±0.1 would overdefine the part: three dimensions for two degrees of freedom, with conflicting limits (the steps allow 39.8 to 40.2), violating single-meaning dimensioning.\n4. Basic: theoretically exact (boxed), tolerance supplied by a geometric tolerance. MAX/MIN: a one-sided limit, only the stated limit applies and the other limit is zero or unlimited.","answers":["(40) = reference dimension, no tolerance, informational","Overall height 39.8 to 40.2 mm","Toleranced overall dimension would overdimension / conflict"]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Which radius symbol for each blend?","q":"A designer needs three radii on one part: (1) a smooth fillet between a shaft and a shoulder that must be tangent to both surfaces with no flats or reversals, (2) a ball end on a lever, which is spherical, and (3) a casting corner where precise shape does not matter. Which symbol, R, CR or SR, is appropriate for each, and what does each one mean for the shape of the surface?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Controlled radius CR: the radius must stay within the two arcs (min and max) and be tangent to adjoining surfaces; flats and reversals are not permitted.\n2. Spherical radius SR: the radius applies to a sphere (all sections through the center show the same radius).\n3. Plain R: a radius whose tolerance zone is bounded by two arcs of the max and min radii; flats and reversals within the zone are allowed if tangent conditions hold, so it fits a non-critical casting corner.","answers":["(1) CR","(2) SR","(3) R"]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Depth of a countersink","q":"A drawing calls out 6X ⌀8.5 THRU with a countersink symbol ⌀16 × 90° on the top face. Find (a) the axial depth of the conical part of the countersink, measured from the top face, ignoring tolerances, and (b) the cone\u0027s included angle versus its half-angle.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Countersink symbol with ⌀ and angle: ⌀16 is the diameter at the surface, 90° is the included angle of the cone, so the half-angle is 45°.\n2. Radial step = (16 − 8.5)/2 = 3.75 mm.\n3. Depth = radial step / tan(half-angle) = 3.75/tan45° = 3.75 mm.","answers":["Countersink depth = 3.75 mm","Included angle 90°, half-angle 45°"]},{"id":"5.1","title":"Which of these are features of size?","q":"Classify each as a feature of size or not: (a) a ⌀30 mm shaft journal, (b) a 12 mm wide milled slot, (c) the top face of a block, (d) a ⌀40 mm spherical ball end, (e) the 50 mm thickness of a plate between two parallel faces. For those that are, say whether the derived element is an axis, a center plane or a center point, and state why this distinction matters for modifiers like MMC.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A feature of size is a feature (or opposed surfaces) associated with a size dimension: it has a size and, being a regular FOS, a derived median element.\n2. (a) Yes, cylinder, derived median line (axis). (b) Yes, two parallel planes, derived median plane (center plane). (c) No, a single planar surface has no size. (d) Yes, sphere, center point. (e) Yes, opposed parallel faces, center plane.\n3. Only features of size can use MMC/LMC (and bonus, virtual condition): material condition requires a size that varies; a plain surface has no MMC.","answers":["(a),(b),(d),(e) features of size; (c) not","Axis for (a), center plane for (b),(e), center point for (d)","MMC/LMC apply only to features of size"]},{"id":"5.2","title":"MMC and LMC for pins and holes","q":"Determine the MMC and LMC sizes for: (a) a hole ⌀8.1 to ⌀8.4 mm, (b) a pin ⌀7.80 to ⌀7.95 mm, (c) a slot 12.0 to 12.3 mm wide, (d) a tab 11.7 to 11.9 mm wide. Then state which size of the hole in (a) gives the most strength of the part and which gives the most material removed.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. MMC = size at which the feature contains the most material; LMC = the least.\n2. Internal features (hole, slot): MMC is the smallest size, LMC the largest. (a) MMC 8.1, LMC 8.4. (c) MMC 12.0, LMC 12.3.\n3. External features (pin, tab): MMC is the largest size, LMC the smallest. (b) MMC 7.95, LMC 7.80. (d) MMC 11.9, LMC 11.7.\n4. The hole at ⌀8.1 (MMC) leaves the most material; ⌀8.4 (LMC) removes the most.","answers":["(a) MMC 8.1, LMC 8.4","(b) MMC 7.95, LMC 7.80","(c) MMC 12.0, LMC 12.3","(d) MMC 11.9, LMC 11.7"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Bowed pin: local size versus mating envelope","q":"A pin is dimensioned ⌀10.00 to ⌀10.20 mm. A produced pin is slightly bowed: every two-point cross-section measures ⌀10.05 mm, but the smallest perfect cylinder that just encloses the whole pin is ⌀10.12 mm. (a) What is the actual local size? (b) What is the actual mating envelope size? (c) Does the pin conform to the size limits under Rule #1? (d) Which of the two sizes is used to compute bonus tolerance, and what bonus does this pin give with an MMC position tolerance?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Actual local size = two-point cross-sectional measurement: 10.05.\n2. Actual mating envelope (unrelated, UAME) for an external feature = smallest perfect cylinder circumscribing the surface: 10.12.\n3. Rule #1: local sizes must be within limits (10.05 ≥ 10.00, ok) and the surface may not violate the perfect-form envelope at MMC ⌀10.20. 10.12 ≤ 10.20, so the pin conforms.\n4. Bonus uses the actual mating envelope, not local size: bonus = |MMC − AME| = 10.20 − 10.12 = 0.08.","answers":["Actual local size 10.05","Actual mating envelope size 10.12","Conforms under Rule #1","Bonus = 0.08 mm (from the mating envelope)"]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Related versus unrelated mating envelope of a hole","q":"A hole is dimensioned ⌀10.0 to ⌀10.2 mm and has a perpendicularity requirement to datum A. The largest perfect cylinder that fits inside the hole with no constraint (unrelated actual mating envelope) is ⌀10.06 mm. The largest perfect cylinder that fits while held exactly perpendicular to datum A (related actual mating envelope) is ⌀10.02 mm. (a) State which envelope is used for a bonus on the perpendicularity tolerance. (b) Compute the bonus if the tolerance is at MMC. (c) Why is the related envelope never larger than the unrelated one for the same hole?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Unrelated AME: not constrained to datums; used for size (Rule #1) assessment. Related AME: constrained in orientation (or location) to the datum reference frame; used when the geometric tolerance relates the feature to datums.\n2. Perpendicularity references datum A, so bonus is computed from the related AME: 10.02.\n3. Hole MMC is the smallest size, 10.0: bonus = actual AME − MMC = 10.02 − 10.0 = 0.02 mm.\n4. A related envelope adds a constraint (orientation/location) to the search, so the best fit can only be equal or smaller than the free (unrelated) fit.","answers":["Related actual mating envelope is used","Bonus = 0.02 mm","Added datum constraint can only shrink the largest fitting cylinder"]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Reading a position feature control frame","q":"A hole\u0027s feature control frame reads, left to right: position symbol | ⌀0.4 Ⓜ | A | B | C. In words, state the characteristic and its category, the tolerance zone shape and size, what the modifier means, the datum precedence and which datum is primary, and which of these items is NOT a datum-related setup requirement for form tolerances like flatness.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Characteristic: position, a location control.\n2. Zone: cylindrical (⌀ symbol), diameter 0.4 mm, centered on the basic location relative to the datums; the hole\u0027s axis must lie inside.\n3. Ⓜ: MMC modifier, the 0.4 applies when the hole is at MMC (smallest size) and grows by bonus as the hole departs toward LMC.\n4. Datum order: A primary, B secondary, C tertiary; the part is set up on A first, then B, then C (the frame reads left to right).\n5. Form tolerances (flatness, straightness, circularity, cylindricity) never take datums; the datum entries apply to orientation, location and runout.","answers":["Position, location control","Cylindrical zone ⌀0.4 at MMC (bonus allowed)","A primary, B secondary, C tertiary","Form tolerances use no datum"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Which characteristics need datums?","q":"Sort these six into the correct category (form, orientation, location, runout, or profile) and state whether a datum reference is required, optional, or forbidden: flatness, perpendicularity, position, circular runout, profile of a surface, cylindricity.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Flatness: form; datums not allowed. Cylindricity: form; datums not allowed.\n2. Perpendicularity: orientation; datum required.\n3. Position: location; datum normally required (the zone is located relative to the datums).\n4. Circular runout: runout; datum axis required.\n5. Profile of a surface: profile; datums optional (unrelated profile controls form/size, related profile controls location/orientation too).\nThe 14 symbols split into form (4), profile (2), orientation (3), location (3), runout (2).","answers":["Flatness, cylindricity: form, no datum","Perpendicularity: orientation, datum required","Position: location, datum required","Circular runout: runout, datum required","Profile of a surface: profile, datum optional"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Find the illegal feature control frames","q":"Three callouts appear on a drawing. (1) A flatness frame on a plate\u0027s top face reads: flatness 0.05 | A. (2) A circularity frame on a pin reads: circularity ⌀0.02. (3) A flatness frame attached by a leader to the surface of a plate (not the size dimension) reads: flatness 0.1 Ⓜ. Say what is wrong with each, and how to fix it.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Flatness is a form control and never references a datum: delete \u0027| A\u0027.\n2. Circularity\u0027s zone is the radial space between two coaxial circles in each cross-section, not a cylinder, so no ⌀ symbol: write circularity 0.02.\n3. MMC (Ⓜ) may be used only with a feature of size (tolerance of a size feature, frame under or attached to the size dimension). A frame on the surface has no material condition, so Ⓜ is illegal: remove Ⓜ (RFS by default), or attach the frame to the width dimension if bonus is wanted.","answers":["(1) Remove datum A: flatness takes no datum","(2) Remove ⌀: circularity zone is annular","(3) Remove Ⓜ or move the frame to the size dimension"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Choosing the correct modifier symbol","q":"For each requirement, name the modifier symbol to use in or next to the feature control frame: (a) a thin, flexible gasket ring whose flatness must be judged in its unrestrained state; (b) a tapped hole where a stud will stand proud of the mating part, so the perpendicularity or position zone must extend beyond the hole\u0027s length; (c) a surface that is uneven and should be located by a tangent plane touching its high points; (d) two coplanar surfaces separated by a slot that must be treated as a single surface.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Free state modifier Ⓕ.\n2. (b) Projected tolerance zone Ⓟ: the zone extends beyond the feature by the stated height (the stud height), so orientation of the stud, not the hole, is controlled.\n3. (c) Tangent plane Ⓣ: the tolerance applies to a plane tangent to the high points of the surface.\n4. (d) The continuous feature symbol, placed next to the frame (older practice was a note such as \u00272 SURFACES\u0027) making the group one feature with a single zone.","answers":["(a) Ⓕ free state","(b) Ⓟ projected tolerance zone","(c) Ⓣ tangent plane","(d) Continuous feature symbol"]},{"id":"7.1","title":"Implied form allowance on a toleranced pin","q":"A ground pin is dimensioned ⌀20.00 to ⌀20.10 mm with no other geometric tolerance, and the drawing invokes ASME Y14.5-2009. (a) What boundary does the drawing impose on the pin\u0027s surface? (b) A produced pin has a constant local diameter of 20.04 mm along its length. What is the largest amount by which its axis may be bowed (as a straightness zone diameter) and still pass? (c) What is that allowance if the pin is produced at 20.00 mm? (d) What does Rule #1 say about the pin\u0027s location relative to other features?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Rule #1 (envelope principle): the surface may not cross a perfect-form boundary at MMC, a ⌀20.10 cylinder over the full length; no local size below 20.00.\n2. Form allowance = MMC − actual local size. At 20.04: 20.10 − 20.04 = 0.06 mm (axis straightness zone ⌀0.06).\n3. At 20.00 (LMC): 20.10 − 20.00 = 0.10 mm.\n4. At MMC (20.10) the pin must be perfect in form.\n5. Rule #1 controls form of an individual feature only; it says nothing about location, orientation, or coaxiality with other features.","answers":["Perfect-form envelope ⌀20.10 over full length; local size ≥ 20.00","0.06 mm allowed bow at 20.04","0.10 mm at 20.00","Rule #1 says nothing about location or orientation"]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Does the envelope principle apply here?","q":"State whether Rule #1 automatically controls the form of the following, and why or why not: (a) a ground steel shaft ⌀19.95 to ⌀20.00 mm; (b) a length of cold-rolled bar stock used as-purchased in a standard size; (c) a thin rubber O-ring whose size is specified in the free state; (d) a pin ⌀10.0 ±0.1 with a straightness tolerance ⌀0.3 applied to its size dimension; (e) a pin ⌀10.0 ±0.1 with the independency symbol next to the size.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Yes: individual regular feature of size, no exception.\n2. (b) No: stock sizes (bar, tube, sheet) follow industry standards, an exception.\n3. (c) No: nonrigid part in the free (unrestrained) state, an exception.\n4. (d) No for the envelope: straightness (or flatness) on the size dimension overrides Rule #1; the tolerance-created boundary (MMC + 0.3) plus local size limits apply.\n5. (e) No: independency overrides Rule #1, only local sizes are checked; form must be added by other tolerances.","answers":["(a) Yes","(b) No (stock)","(c) No (free state nonrigid)","(d) Overridden by straightness on size","(e) Overridden by independency"]},{"id":"7.3","title":"Hole position with no modifier on the frame","q":"A hole is ⌀6.0 to ⌀6.1 mm with position ⌀0.2 mm relative to datum A (a surface), with no modifier in the tolerance cell. (a) What does Rule #2 say about this frame? (b) If the hole is produced at ⌀6.1, what position tolerance applies? (c) If the frame had Ⓜ after ⌀0.2, what would apply at ⌀6.1? (d) A datum B feature of size is referenced in a different frame with no modifier; how is it simulated?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Rule #2: with no modifier, RFS applies to the tolerance and RMB to datum references.\n2. RFS: position tolerance stays ⌀0.2 at any produced size, including ⌀6.1: no bonus.\n3. With Ⓜ: hole MMC = 6.0; at 6.1 departure from MMC = 0.1, so total tolerance = 0.2 + 0.1 = ⌀0.3.\n4. Datum feature of size with no modifier: RMB, so the datum is established by the actual mating envelope of the feature (e.g. an expanding/centering simulator) regardless of its size.","answers":["No modifier means RFS for the tolerance and RMB for datums","⌀0.2 at ⌀6.1 (no bonus)","⌀0.3 at ⌀6.1 with Ⓜ","Datum B is RMB: simulator contacts the feature, adjusting to its size"]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Gage sizes for a hole under Rule #1","q":"A through-hole is ⌀8.00 to ⌀8.06 mm, 25 mm long, with Rule #1 applying and no other tolerance. Specify the size and length of the GO gage and the size of the NOGO gage, state what each checks, how the NOGO gage is used differently from the GO gage, and what it means if the GO gage fails to enter.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. GO gage (pin): made at MMC = smallest hole = ⌀8.00, at least as long as the feature (25 mm). It must enter the full length: this checks MMC size and Rule #1 perfect-form envelope over the full length.\n2. NOGO gage (pin): made at LMC = ⌀8.06; it must not enter. It checks the actual local size (cross-section by cross-section): used at each end and in several sections, not just once.\n3. If the GO gage fails to enter, a portion of the hole is smaller than MMC or violates the perfect-form envelope (e.g. bent/tapered/barrel shape): reject.\n4. Fixed-limit gages give pass/fail only, not a measurement.","answers":["GO pin ⌀8.00 at least 25 long: must enter full length","NOGO pin ⌀8.06: must not enter, checked cross-section by cross-section at both ends","GO failing means MMC/envelope violation"]},{"id":"8.1","title":"Virtual condition and bonus for a position callout","q":"A hole is ⌀13.0 to ⌀13.4 mm with position ⌀0.5 Ⓜ relative to datums A, B, C. (a) Compute the virtual condition. (b) A produced hole measures ⌀13.25 mm (actual mating envelope): what is the bonus and the total position tolerance? (c) What is the maximum total position tolerance, and at what hole size? (d) What size should the functional gage pin be?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Internal feature: MMC = smallest = 13.0. VC = MMC − geometric tolerance = 13.0 − 0.5 = ⌀12.5.\n2. Bonus = |actual mating envelope − MMC| = 13.25 − 13.0 = 0.25. Total = 0.5 + 0.25 = ⌀0.75.\n3. Max bonus = MMC − LMC = 0.4 at ⌀13.4, so max total = 0.5 + 0.4 = ⌀0.9.\n4. Functional gage: fixed pin at VC ⌀12.5, located by the basic dimensions from the datum reference frame; the hole must pass over it. Size limits are checked separately.","answers":["VC = ⌀12.5","Bonus 0.25, total ⌀0.75 at 13.25","Max total ⌀0.9 at ⌀13.4","Gage pin ⌀12.5"]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Straightness on a shaft\u0027s size dimension","q":"A shaft is ⌀15.0 to ⌀15.2 mm. In one case there is no geometric tolerance. In another, the frame straightness ⌀0.1 Ⓜ is placed under the size dimension. (a) Give the outer boundary in each case. (b) For the second case, what total straightness tolerance is allowed on a shaft produced at ⌀15.05, and at ⌀15.0? (c) Does the second callout change the size limits?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. No geometric tolerance: worst-case outer boundary = Rule #1 MMC envelope = ⌀15.2.\n2. Straightness at MMC on the size overrides Rule #1; the boundary is the virtual condition = MMC + tolerance = 15.2 + 0.1 = ⌀15.3.\n3. Bonus at 15.05 = 15.2 − 15.05 = 0.15, total = 0.25. At 15.0 (LMC): bonus 0.2, total 0.3.\n4. Size limits 15.0 to 15.2 still apply to every local size; the bow can now exceed the Rule #1 allowance, up to the VC.","answers":["Outer boundary ⌀15.2 (no tolerance) versus ⌀15.3 (VC)","Total straightness 0.25 at 15.05; 0.3 at 15.0","Size limits unchanged"]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Sizing mating hole and pin position tolerances","q":"A pin of ⌀8.0 to ⌀8.2 mm carries position ⌀0.3 Ⓜ to its datum frame. It must assemble into a hole ⌀8.7 to ⌀9.0 mm that also has a position tolerance at MMC. (a) Compute the pin\u0027s virtual condition. (b) What is the maximum hole position tolerance that still guarantees assembly in the worst case? (c) What is the clearance when both parts are at their virtual conditions with this tolerance? (d) If the hole position were ⌀0.3 Ⓜ instead, what happens in the worst case?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Pin VC = MMC + tol = 8.2 + 0.3 = ⌀8.5.\n2. Hole VC = MMC − tol = 8.7 − t. Assembly needs hole VC ≥ pin VC: 8.7 − t ≥ 8.5, so t ≤ 0.2 mm. (Same as fixed-fastener rule: H − F = 8.7 − 8.2 = 0.5 = T_H + T_P = 0.2 + 0.3.)\n3. With t = 0.2, hole VC = 8.5 = pin VC: zero clearance, just assembles in the worst case.\n4. Hole position 0.3: VC = 8.4 \u003c 8.5, a diametral interference of 0.1 is possible in the worst case: assembly not guaranteed.","answers":["Pin VC ⌀8.5","Max hole position tolerance ⌀0.2 at MMC","Worst-case clearance zero","With ⌀0.3 interference of 0.1 is possible"]},{"id":"8.4","title":"Judge three claims about bonus tolerance","q":"Hole: ⌀10.00 to ⌀10.10 mm. A colleague makes three claims. Claim A: with position ⌀0.2 Ⓜ, a hole produced at ⌀10.06 is allowed ⌀0.26 position tolerance. Claim B: with the bonus, the hole\u0027s size limits can be extended to ⌀10.00 to ⌀10.16. Claim C: a profile of a surface tolerance of 0.2 on the same hole also gets bonus. Evaluate each, and say what happens to claim A if the frame has no modifier.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A: correct. Hole MMC = 10.00; bonus = 10.06 − 10.00 = 0.06; total = 0.2 + 0.06 = 0.26.\n2. B: wrong. Bonus is added to the geometric tolerance, never to the size tolerance; limits stay 10.00 to 10.10.\n3. C: wrong. Bonus is permitted only for flatness, straightness, orientation and position on features of size with Ⓜ or Ⓛ; not for profile, circularity, cylindricity, symmetry, concentricity or runout.\n4. With no modifier (RFS by Rule #2) there is no bonus: the tolerance stays ⌀0.2 at every size.","answers":["A correct: ⌀0.26","B wrong: bonus never adds to size tolerance","C wrong: no bonus for profile","RFS: no bonus"]},{"id":"9.1","title":"Reading and inspecting a surface flatness callout","q":"The top face of a plate is dimensioned 20.0 ±0.2 mm thick, with a flatness frame of 0.08 mm leadered to the top face (no datums). (a) Describe the tolerance zone. (b) Is the value legal relative to the size tolerance? (c) A surface-plate inspection, with the part rested on its high points and the indicator zeroed at plate level, gives these deviations at five points: +0.03, −0.02, +0.05, −0.04, +0.01 mm. Does the surface pass? (d) What can the surface do that the zone still allows?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.08 apart, free in orientation and location; all points on the surface must lie between them.\n2. Rule #1 applies to the thickness (0.4 total), so a surface flatness must be smaller than the size tolerance: 0.08 \u003c 0.4, legal and a true refinement.\n3. Flatness = max − min of deviations = 0.05 − (−0.04) = 0.09 mm \u003e 0.08, so it fails (thickness limits 19.8 to 20.2 are checked separately).\n4. The zone allows any waviness, warp, tilt or crown within 0.08.","answers":["Two parallel planes 0.08 apart, no datum","Legal: 0.08 \u003c 0.4","Measured flatness 0.09 \u003e 0.08: fails","Waviness and warp allowed within the zone"]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Flatness allowed with no flatness callout","q":"A plate is dimensioned 15.0 to 15.4 mm thick, nothing else. Rule #1 applies. (a) What is the largest allowable flatness deviation of the plate\u0027s derived median plane at a produced local size of 15.15 mm? (b) At 15.0 mm? (c) What is it at 15.4 mm? (d) Why might you still add a flatness callout on one surface?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Rule #1 envelope: external width, MMC = largest = 15.4: the plate must fit between two parallel planes 15.4 apart.\n2. Allowed deviation = MMC − actual local size: at 15.15: 0.25 mm; at 15.0 (LMC): 0.4 mm (the full size tolerance); at 15.4: 0 (perfect form).\n3. Flatness is thus indirectly limited by the size tolerance.\n4. A direct flatness callout is justified when function (sealing, support, measurement repeatability) needs flatter than the size tolerance allows, or when you want flatness actually inspected; it must then be less than 0.4.","answers":["0.25 mm at 15.15","0.4 mm at 15.0","0 at 15.4 (perfect form)","Add one only to refine tighter than the size tolerance"]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Flatness at MMC on a tab width","q":"A tab is dimensioned 8.00 to 8.30 mm wide, with the frame flatness 0.2 Ⓜ placed under the size dimension. (a) What is the virtual condition? (b) At a produced size of 8.12 mm (actual mating envelope), what total flatness tolerance applies to the derived median plane? (c) At 8.00 mm? (d) Describe a functional gage and say what happens to Rule #1.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. External width: VC = MMC + flatness tol = 8.30 + 0.2 = 8.50.\n2. Bonus at 8.12 = 8.30 − 8.12 = 0.18; total = 0.2 + 0.18 = 0.38 mm.\n3. At 8.00 (LMC): bonus 0.30; total = 0.50 mm (= 8.50 − 8.00).\n4. Rule #1 is overridden (flatness on a size), so the form allowance may exceed the 0.30 size tolerance; size limits still apply.\n5. Gage: a full-length slot 8.50 wide; the tab must pass through. Local sizes are checked separately.","answers":["VC 8.50 mm","0.38 mm at 8.12","0.50 mm at 8.00","Rule #1 overridden; gage slot 8.50"]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Which flatness callouts are correct?","q":"A plate is dimensioned 10.0 to 10.2 mm thick. Three callouts are proposed: (1) flatness 0.3 leadered to a surface; (2) flatness 0.3 placed under the 10.0 to 10.2 size dimension; (3) flatness 0.05 | A leadered to a surface. For each, say whether it is acceptable and, if so, what it means, quoting any outer boundary.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) Not useful: Rule #1 applies to the thickness, so a surface flatness must be smaller than the size tolerance (0.2). 0.3 is larger and cannot refine anything; Rule #1 already limits flatness to at most 0.2.\n2. (2) Acceptable: flatness on a feature of size overrides Rule #1; the derived median plane must lie in a zone 0.3 wide, may exceed the size tolerance. RFS: outer boundary = MMC + 0.3 = 10.5, no bonus; size limits 10.0 to 10.2 still apply.\n3. (3) Illegal: flatness is a form tolerance and never takes a datum reference; write flatness 0.05.","answers":["(1) Not valid: exceeds the size tolerance with Rule #1 in force","(2) Valid: median plane zone 0.3, outer boundary 10.5, Rule #1 overridden","(3) Illegal: no datum on flatness"]},{"id":"10.1","title":"Straightness Rule #1 already allows on a pin","q":"A round pin is dimensioned ⌀16.0 to 16.6 mm. Rule #1 applies and there is no straightness frame. (a) What is the largest deviation of the pin\u0027s derived median line from straight if the produced local size is everywhere 16.6? (b) If it is everywhere 16.3? (c) If it is 16.0? (d) A designer proposes adding a straightness frame of 0.2 leadered to the surface of the pin. Is that legal, and what does it add?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Rule #1: perfect form at MMC (16.6); the pin must fit a 16.6 perfect-form envelope. Allowed deviation = MMC − actual size.\n2. (a) 0 mm. (b) 16.6 − 16.3 = 0.3 mm. (c) 16.6 − 16.0 = 0.6 mm, the full size tolerance.\n3. (d) A leadered frame on the surface controls each line element in a zone of two parallel lines 0.2 apart; no ⌀. It must be smaller than the size tolerance (0.6) because Rule #1 applies: 0.2 \u003c 0.6, legal. It refines straightness of each surface line element to 0.2 at any size and makes it an inspected requirement; the MMC envelope and size limits still apply separately.","answers":["(a) 0; (b) 0.3 mm; (c) 0.6 mm","Frame 0.2 on the surface is legal (0.2 \u003c 0.6): two parallel lines 0.2 apart on each element"]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Pin straightness at MMC: boundary and bonus","q":"A pin is dimensioned ⌀12.0 to 12.3 mm with a straightness frame ⌀0.1 Ⓜ placed under the size dimension. (a) Describe the effect on Rule #1. (b) What is the virtual condition? (c) What total straightness tolerance applies to the median line at a produced size of 12.15? At 12.0? (d) Describe the functional gage.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Straightness at MMC on a feature of size overrides Rule #1, so the form allowance may exceed the size tolerance (0.3); size limits still apply and are two-point checked.\n2. (b) External: VC = MMC + tol = 12.3 + 0.1 = 12.4.\n3. (c) Bonus = 12.3 − 12.15 = 0.15; total = 0.1 + 0.15 = 0.25. At 12.0: bonus 0.3, total 0.4 (= 12.4 − 12.0).\n4. (d) A hole of ⌀12.4, at least as long as the pin; the pin must enter it. No datums, so the gage hole just checks form.","answers":["Rule #1 overridden","VC ⌀12.4","0.25 at 12.15; 0.4 at 12.0","Full-length ⌀12.4 hole gage"]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Which straightness callouts are wrong?","q":"For a ⌀20 ±0.1 shaft and a 10 ±0.1 wide key, four straightness callouts are proposed: (1) straightness 0.05 | A on the shaft surface; (2) straightness 0.02 leadered to the shaft surface; (3) straightness ⌀0.05 under the 10 ±0.1 key width; (4) straightness 0.3 leadered to the shaft surface. Rule #1 applies. For each, say whether it is legal and, if not, why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) Illegal: straightness is a form tolerance and never takes a datum reference.\n2. (2) Legal: line elements, two parallel lines 0.02 apart, 0.02 \u003c size tolerance 0.2.\n3. (3) Illegal: a straightness frame may not be applied to a width (planar) feature of size, and ⌀ is for cylindrical features only. Flatness would be used for a width.\n4. (4) Not valid under Rule #1: 0.3 is larger than the size tolerance 0.2, so it refines nothing (Rule #1 already limits it to at most 0.2); it would only be meaningful if Rule #1 were overridden.","answers":["(1) illegal, datum on form","(2) legal","(3) illegal, width feature","(4) not valid, exceeds size tolerance"]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Inspecting a hole against a straightness MMC callout","q":"A hole is dimensioned ⌀8.0 to 8.2 mm with straightness ⌀0.05 Ⓜ under the size. (a) What is the virtual condition? (b) What total straightness applies at a produced size of 8.12? (c) Inspection finds the hole at 8.12 with its derived median line bowed within a ⌀0.18 cylinder. Does it pass? (d) What straightness tolerance is allowed at LMC?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Internal: MMC = smallest = 8.0; VC = MMC − tol = 8.0 − 0.05 = 7.95.\n2. (b) Bonus = 8.12 − 8.0 = 0.12; total = 0.05 + 0.12 = 0.17.\n3. (c) 0.18 \u003e 0.17: fails (the hole would not accept a ⌀7.95 gage pin).\n4. (d) At LMC 8.2: bonus 0.2, total 0.25.","answers":["VC ⌀7.95","0.17 at 8.12","Fails: 0.18 \u003e 0.17","0.25 at 8.2"]},{"id":"11.1","title":"Reading a circularity callout and checking a trace","q":"A journal is dimensioned ⌀10.0 to 10.2 mm with a circularity frame of 0.02 leadered to its surface. (a) Describe the tolerance zone and say whether a datum or ⌀ is allowed. (b) A roundness trace at one cross section, with the zone circles fitted about a common center, shows a high point at radius 5.060 and a low point at radius 5.035. Does that section pass? (c) A second journal is perfectly round at every section but has a 0.05 mm diametral taper end to end. Does it fail circularity?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Two coaxial circles 0.02 apart radially, in each cross section independently; size of the circles is free. No datum, no ⌀ (the value is a radial width).\n2. (b) Radial gap = 5.060 − 5.035 = 0.025 \u003e 0.02: fails.\n3. (c) Circularity looks at each section on its own, so a taper with perfect circles does not violate it; taper is limited by size limits / Rule #1 (and cylindricity if specified).","answers":["Two coaxial circles 0.02 radially apart, per section; no datum, no ⌀","0.025 \u003e 0.02: fails","Taper with round sections passes circularity"]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Circularity versus the size tolerance","q":"A plug is dimensioned ⌀25.00 to 25.05 mm with Rule #1 in force. (a) What is the greatest out-of-roundness Rule #1 can permit, and at what size? (b) A circularity frame of 0.06 is added; is it legal? (c) A frame of 0.01 is added; what does it change? (d) In what situation could a circularity value larger than the size tolerance be legal?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Rule #1 forces perfect form at MMC (25.05); allowed deviation grows as size falls, max at LMC 25.00 = the size tolerance = 0.05 mm.\n2. (b) 0.06 \u003e 0.05: not a refinement, not valid with Rule #1.\n3. (c) 0.01 requires every cross section to fit in two coaxial circles 0.01 radially apart at any size, much tighter than Rule #1 permits (and gets inspected).\n4. (d) Only where Rule #1 is overridden (independency symbol, stock size); size limits still must be met.","answers":["0.05 mm, at LMC","0.06 not legal","0.01 tightens each section\u0027s roundness","Legal only if Rule #1 is overridden"]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Spotting illegal circularity frames","q":"Four circularity callouts on a ⌀30 ±0.1 shaft are proposed: (1) ⌀0.02 in the frame; (2) 0.02 with a Ⓜ modifier; (3) 0.02 | A; (4) 0.02 attached to the ⌀30 ±0.1 size dimension line. State what is wrong with each.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) ⌀ not allowed: the zone is a radial annulus, not a cylinder.\n2. (2) Ⓜ not allowed: only free state and statistical modifiers are permitted; circularity gives no bonus tolerance.\n3. (3) Datum not allowed: circularity is a form control on a single feature.\n4. (4) The frame must be leadered to the surface (or extension line), applied to the surface elements, not the size dimension.","answers":["⌀ illegal","Ⓜ illegal","Datum illegal","Must go on surface, not the size dimension"]},{"id":"11.4","title":"Choosing roundness control for a seal bushing","q":"A thin bushing must seal against a rotating shaft. Function requires each cross section of its bore (⌀40.00 to 40.04) to be round within 0.01, but the designer is willing to accept a slight taper along the bore and some bend of its axis. Which form control is appropriate, how would you write it and what does it allow? Why is cylindricity a poorer choice?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Circularity 0.01 leadered to the bore surface, no datum, no ⌀: each cross section between two coaxial circles 0.01 apart radially.\n2. It allows taper and axis bend, as required, since each section\u0027s circles may be any size and center.\n3. 0.01 \u003c 0.04 size tolerance, so it refines Rule #1.\n4. Cylindricity ties all sections to one common axis and would needlessly forbid taper and bend beyond 0.01 radially.","answers":["Circularity 0.01, leader to surface, no datum","Allows taper/bend, controls each section","Cylindricity too restrictive"]},{"id":"12.1","title":"Taper against a cylindricity zone","q":"A shaft is dimensioned ⌀10.0 to 10.2 mm with a cylindricity frame of 0.02 leadered to its surface. (a) Describe the zone. (b) One shaft has perfectly round sections and a perfectly straight axis, but its diameter is 10.14 at one end and 10.10 at the other, varying linearly. Does it pass cylindricity? (c) Another is the same but 10.16 at one end and 10.10 at the other. Does it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Two coaxial cylinders 0.02 apart radially, containing the whole surface at once; no datum, no ⌀.\n2. (b) Diameter change 0.04 → radius change 0.02. The best coaxial cylinder pair must be 0.02 apart radially: 0.02 ≤ 0.02, passes.\n3. (c) Diameter change 0.06 → radial 0.03 \u003e 0.02: fails.\n4. Sizes remain within 10.0–10.2 in both cases.","answers":["Two coaxial cylinders 0.02 apart radially","10.14/10.10: passes (0.02 radial)","10.16/10.10: fails (0.03 radial)"]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Circularity or cylindricity for a bent shaft","q":"A ground shaft ⌀25.00 to 25.02 is checked. Every cross section is within 0.003 of perfectly round, but the shaft axis is bowed so that the surface lies within two coaxial cylinders 0.015 apart radially at best. (a) Does the shaft pass a circularity of 0.005? (b) Does it pass a cylindricity of 0.005? (c) What is the maximum cylindricity Rule #1 could permit, and so what upper limit makes a cylindricity callout a refinement?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Circularity is evaluated per section: 0.003 ≤ 0.005, passes; bow is ignored.\n2. (b) Cylindricity governs the whole surface about one axis: 0.015 \u003e 0.005, fails.\n3. (c) Rule #1 permits at most the size tolerance = 0.02; a cylindricity value must be less than 0.02 to refine it.","answers":["Circularity passes","Cylindricity fails (0.015 \u003e 0.005)","Max by Rule #1 0.02; callout must be \u003c 0.02"]},{"id":"12.3","title":"Form allowed at an intermediate size by Rule #1","q":"A bar is dimensioned ⌀40.00 to 40.04 mm, Rule #1 applies, and a cylindricity of 0.015 is also specified. A produced part measures 40.01 at every two-point section. (a) How much form error does Rule #1 alone allow at this size? (b) Which of the two requirements actually limits the part? (c) Would a cylindricity of 0.05 have been acceptable on the drawing?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) MMC = 40.04; allowed deviation = 40.04 − 40.01 = 0.03 (a diametral envelope allowance).\n2. (b) The cylindricity value 0.015 is smaller, so it governs (radial zone 0.015); the Rule #1 envelope must still be met.\n3. (c) No: 0.05 exceeds the size tolerance 0.04 and cannot refine Rule #1.","answers":["0.03 from Rule #1","Cylindricity 0.015 governs","0.05 not acceptable (\u003e 0.04)"]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Find the errors in cylindricity callouts","q":"Four cylindricity callouts are proposed for a ⌀12 ±0.05 pin: (1) 0.01 | A; (2) ⌀0.01 in the frame; (3) 0.01 Ⓜ; (4) 0.01 placed under the ⌀12 ±0.05 size dimension. Say what is wrong in each and what correct application looks like.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) Datum not allowed on a form tolerance.\n2. (2) ⌀ not allowed; the zone is two coaxial cylinders, the value is a radial spacing.\n3. (3) Material condition modifiers not allowed; only free state and statistical.\n4. (4) Cylindricity applies to the surface, so the leader goes to the surface or extension line, not the size dimension.\n5. Correct: frame with the symbol and 0.01 (\u003c 0.1 size tolerance) leadered to the surface.","answers":["Datum illegal","⌀ illegal","Ⓜ illegal","Must be on the surface, not the size"]},{"id":"13.1","title":"Degrees of freedom left after each datum","q":"A rectangular bracket is positioned using datum A (large mounting face), datum B (a side face) and datum C (an end face), in that order, all planar. (a) How many degrees of freedom does each datum constrain, and which? (b) What is the minimum number of contact points on each (3-2-1)? (c) Why must the angles between A, B and C be toleranced on the drawing? (d) Does the 3-2-1 rule apply if datum B is a hole?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) A: 3 DOF (1 translation normal to A, 2 rotations about axes in A). B: 2 more (1 rotation about the normal of A, 1 translation normal to B). C: 1 more (translation normal to C). Total 6.\n2. (b) A: 3 points, B: 2 points, C: 1 point.\n3. (c) Theoretical datum planes are exactly 90°, real faces are not; the contact sequence plus a tolerance on the angles makes the result repeatable.\n4. (d) No: 3-2-1 applies only when all datums are planar.","answers":["A 3, B 2, C 1 DOF","3-2-1 contact points","Real faces are not exactly 90°: toleranced","3-2-1 only for planar datums"]},{"id":"13.2","title":"Reading a datum reference frame from a callout","q":"A flange has a position frame: ⌀0.5 Ⓜ | A | B Ⓜ. A is the machined mounting face and B is the pilot diameter. (a) Which datum contacts the simulator first and what does it constrain? (b) What does B add? (c) Why is no tertiary datum needed for an equally spaced bolt circle? (d) Distinguish datum feature, datum feature simulator and datum.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) A is primary: the face rests on the simulator (≥3 contacts), orienting the part; 3 DOF.\n2. (b) B is secondary: the pilot axis (perpendicular to A), 2 translations; 5 DOF in total, rotation about axis B left.\n3. (c) The equally spaced pattern is basically dimensioned about B, so rotation about the axis does not alter the zone locations.\n4. (d) Datum feature: the real part surface or FOS identified by the symbol. Simulator: the perfect boundary (theoretical) or real gage/plate that contacts it. Datum: the theoretically exact plane/axis/point derived from the simulator.","answers":["A primary, 3 DOF","B secondary, 2 translations","Pattern symmetric, no clocking datum","Definitions of feature, simulator, datum"]},{"id":"13.3","title":"Why implied datums fail and what fixes rocking","q":"An inspector measures a hole position on a cast plate whose underside is slightly convex, using whichever surfaces seem convenient (implied datums). (a) Give two shortcomings of implied datums. (b) What happens to repeatability? (c) How do you fix the rocking problem for the primary datum feature on the drawing? (d) What real physical object plays the role of the datum feature simulator for a planar primary?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The drawing does not say which surfaces contact the equipment, nor in what order; different setups give different results.\n2. (b) Good parts may be rejected and bad accepted; results do not repeat between inspectors.\n3. (c) Identify the surface as a datum feature in a DRF and control its form, e.g. flatness, or use datum targets (3 points for a primary) so the part sits stably.\n4. (d) A surface plate or granite table, simulating the tangent plane contacting the high points.","answers":["Order/contact unspecified","Poor repeatability","Flatness or targets","Surface plate"]},{"id":"13.4","title":"Spot datum system errors on a drawing","q":"A drawing has the following: datum feature symbols A and C (no B); datum D is attached to a center line of a hole; a datum E symbol that is never referenced in any frame; and a length dimension 120 ±0.5 measured from datum A with the part clamped in the datum reference frame. Identify the error in each and the correct practice.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Letters must be assigned without skipping: A, B, ...\n2. A datum feature symbol is never attached to a center line; attach it to the size dimension or to the feature surface.\n3. A datum feature never referenced in any frame is meaningless and should be deleted.\n4. Size dimensions (length, width, height) are not datum-related; measure them without fixturing in the DRF, otherwise orientation error can reject a good part.","answers":["No skipped letters","Not on a centre line","Remove unused datum","Sizes are measured outside the DRF"]},{"id":"14.1","title":"Interpreting datum target symbols on a casting","q":"A rough casting has datum targets: A1, A2, A3 (three points) on the bottom face, B1, B2 (two points) on one side face, and C1 (one point) on an end face. (a) What do the two halves of a datum target symbol contain? (b) What does a solid versus a dashed leader mean? (c) Which datum is primary, and how are the target locations and sizes normally specified? (d) How many degrees of freedom do the targets constrain?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Bottom half: datum letter and target number (A1); top half: target area size when applicable.\n2. (b) Solid leader: target on the visible (near) side; dashed: on the hidden side.\n3. (c) Primary is A (first in the frame), 3 points; locations/sizes given by basic dimensions (standard practice, minimizes gage-to-gage variation).\n4. (d) 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 DOF.","answers":["Letter+number bottom, size on top","Solid near side, dashed far side","A primary; basic dimensions","6 DOF"]},{"id":"14.2","title":"When to choose datum targets and what shape","q":"A rough sand-cast base with a warped bottom face must be machined and inspected repeatably. (a) Give two reasons to use datum targets rather than the whole face as primary datum feature. (b) For the primary datum, how many targets are needed and what gage elements simulate a point, a line and an area target? (c) Why would a target-only primary datum not be repeatable by itself?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The whole surface is impractical or would rock on the plate; only part of the feature is functional; targets give one repeatable orientation.\n2. (b) Three points for a planar primary. Point: spherical-tipped pin; line: side of a cylindrical pin (tangent); area: flat-tipped pin matching the area shape.\n3. (c) A primary from targets constrains only 3 DOF; secondary and tertiary datum references are needed to constrain all six.","answers":["Warp/rocking, partial function","3 points; sphere tip, pin side, flat pin","Secondary/tertiary needed"]},{"id":"14.3","title":"Movable datum targets on a round casting","q":"A round boss is located in a gage by two area targets B1 and B2 on its outside at 90° to each other forming a V that centres it. (a) What does the movable datum target symbol (pointed circle) mean, and in what direction does the simulator move by default? (b) Why would fixed simulators at basic locations be wrong for this boss? (c) Name the cases in which target simulators are movable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The simulator of that target is movable (adjustable): it moves normal to the part surface by default; any other direction must be specified.\n2. (b) Boss diameter varies within its tolerance; fixed simulators at basic positions would shift the datum axis off the part or make it not fit; movable V targets close on the surface and centre it.\n3. (c) Targets on a feature of size at RMB, the movable target symbol shown, or a translation modifier used.","answers":["Simulator moves, normal to surface by default","Size variation needs adjustable simulators","RMB FOS, movable symbol or translation modifier"]},{"id":"14.4","title":"Spot errors in a datum target scheme","q":"A drawing shows: a primary datum from only two point targets A1, A2; target locations dimensioned with plus/minus tolerances; leader lines with arrowheads; and a datum line target shown only in an edge view with an X. Identify what is wrong or discouraged in each.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A planar primary needs three targets (3 points).\n2. Y14.5 permits toleranced targets but basic dimensions are standard practice, giving less gage-to-gage variation.\n3. Datum target leader lines have no arrowhead.\n4. A line target shown only in an edge view with an X looks like a point target; use the phantom line in the plan view (with the X in the edge view).","answers":["Primary needs 3 points","Use basic dimensions","No arrowheads","Line target needs plan-view phantom line"]},{"id":"15.1","title":"Shaft as primary datum feature with no modifier","q":"A stepped shaft has its ⌀20.0 to 20.1 mm journal labelled datum A, and a hole elsewhere on the part has position ⌀0.1 | A (no modifier on A). (a) What is the datum feature simulator, and is its size fixed? (b) How many degrees of freedom does A constrain and which? (c) A produced journal has straight, round sections of 20.04 to 20.06 but is bowed so that its unrelated actual mating envelope is ⌀20.08. What simulator size is used? (d) Where must the datum feature symbol be attached, and where never?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) RMB is the default: an adjustable simulator (collet, chuck, expanding mandrel) that contacts the high points; it simulates the unrelated actual mating envelope (smallest circumscribed cylinder). Size not fixed.\n2. (b) 4 DOF: two translations and two rotations; not translation along or rotation about the axis.\n3. (c) The simulator closes to the UAME, ⌀20.08; the datum axis is its axis. (A bowed journal still must meet size limits and Rule #1 envelope of 20.1.)\n4. (d) On/with the size dimension (in line with the dimension line, on the surface or a leader extension, or on the feature\u0027s own frame); never on a center line.","answers":["Adjustable simulator = UAME","4 DOF","⌀20.08","On the size dimension/surface, never a centre line"]},{"id":"15.2","title":"Building a DRF from a face, a bore and a slot","q":"A plate has mounting face A (primary), a central bore B (⌀30.0 to 30.1, secondary, RMB) and a slot C (width 8.0 to 8.2, tertiary, RMB). A position frame on a hole pattern reads ⌀0.2 | A | B | C. (a) What does each datum constrain, with the running total of DOF? (b) Describe the simulators for B and C. (c) What would be lost if C were omitted, and why does the order of B and C matter?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) A plane: 3 DOF (1 translation, 2 rotations). B bore axis, perpendicular to A: 2 translations, total 5. C center plane: rotation about axis B, total 6.\n2. (b) B: adjustable cylinder, perpendicular to A, expanding to the related actual mating envelope (smallest inscribed for a hole). C: adjustable parallel planes, perpendicular to A and centered on axis B, closing to the slot\u0027s high points (width RMB), clocking the part.\n3. (c) Without C the pattern could rotate freely about B so its angular position would be unrepeatable. Order matters: B (position) is established before C, which is oriented to B and A, not vice versa.","answers":["A 3, B +2 (=5), C +1 (=6)","B adjustable axis simulator, C adjustable center plane","C locks rotation about B"]},{"id":"15.3","title":"Coaxial journals as a single datum","q":"A spindle has two short coaxial journals, ⌀20.00 to 20.02 and ⌀30.00 to 30.02, 80 mm apart. A circular runout 0.04 is called out to A-B. (a) What does A-B mean at RMB? (b) How many DOF does it constrain? (c) What would be wrong with A primary and B secondary instead? (d) Describe the physical simulator.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The two features are one datum feature of size: a common datum axis from simulators that expand/contract about a common center and contact both journals simultaneously.\n2. (b) 4 DOF: two translations, two rotations (a single primary).\n3. (c) Each journal is too short alone to orient the part; as A primary a short journal gives poor orientation and B only a point of contact, so the axis would depend on arbitrary rocking.\n4. (d) e.g. two coaxial adjustable collets or V-blocks tied to a common spindle (ideally two chucks closing together on both diameters).","answers":["One datum axis from both journals","4 DOF","A-B single co-datum prevents rocking","Two coaxial adjustable simulators"]},{"id":"15.4","title":"Are these datum choices sensible?","q":"A sheet-metal stamping, 2 mm thick, has a 6 mm hole proposed as primary datum feature A RMB, and a long ⌀25 shaft proposed as secondary. A reviewer also notes that the datum feature symbol for a hole was attached to its center line. A V-block is proposed as the RMB simulator of the shaft. Give the problems and a better scheme.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A short hole in thin stock cannot orient the part: the primary needs enough length to constrain 2 rotations, so it is a poor primary.\n2. Better: a flat face as primary (3 points), then the hole as secondary (2 translations).\n3. The datum feature symbol must go on the size dimension or surface, never the center line.\n4. A V-block or 3-point contact does not equal the minimum circumscribed or maximum inscribed cylinder; use an adjustable collet/chuck-type simulator for RMB.","answers":["Short hole poor primary","Use face primary, hole secondary","Symbol not on centre line","V-block not a valid RMB simulator"]},{"id":"16.1","title":"MMB of a primary shaft and the shift it gives","q":"A shaft datum feature A is dimensioned ⌀16.0 to 16.6 mm, Rule #1 applies, and it is referenced as A Ⓜ (primary) in the frame of a coaxial hub: position ⌀0.2 | A Ⓜ. (a) What is the MMB, and what is its simulator? (b) If instead the shaft carried straightness ⌀0.4 Ⓜ, what would the MMB be? (c) A produced shaft has an actual mating envelope of ⌀16.3. What datum feature shift is allowed (diametral terms), and how does it affect the hub\u0027s allowed coaxiality? (d) How many DOF does the primary cylinder constrain?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) With Rule #1 in force MMB = MMC = 16.6: a fixed-size ⌀16.6 hole/bore simulator (datum axis = its axis).\n2. (b) Straightness at MMC overrides Rule #1: MMB = 16.6 + 0.4 = 17.0.\n3. (c) Shift = MMB − actual envelope = 16.6 − 16.3 = 0.3 diametral (±0.15 radially); the whole hub\u0027s axis may move relative to the shaft axis by that extra amount, so the effective coaxiality is 0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5 (diametral), before any hub bonus.\n4. (d) 4 DOF (two translations, two rotations).","answers":["MMB 16.6","MMB 17.0 with straightness","Shift 0.3 (diametral); total 0.5 effective","4 DOF"]},{"id":"16.2","title":"MMB of a secondary hole and slot","q":"A plate has primary face A, a hole B dimensioned ⌀50 ±0.2 with perpendicularity ⌀0.1 Ⓜ | A, and a slot C of width 6.1 to 6.3 with position 0.05 Ⓜ | A | B Ⓜ. (a) Compute the MMB for B as a secondary datum feature. (b) Compute the MMB of the slot if later used as a datum at MMB. (c) A produced hole B has an actual mating envelope of 50.1. How much shift is available? (d) Why is a geometric tolerance relating B to A required?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Internal: MMC = 49.8; MMB = MMC − perpendicularity = 49.8 − 0.1 = 49.7.\n2. (b) Slot internal MMC = 6.1; MMB = 6.1 − 0.05 = 6.05.\n3. (c) Shift = actual size − MMB = 50.1 − 49.7 = 0.4 (diametral), usable as part movement within the fixed ⌀49.7 pin.\n4. (d) A secondary MMB simulator is fixed in size and orientation to A; without a tolerance tying B to A the MMB is undefined (here the perpendicularity).","answers":["49.7","6.05","Shift 0.4","Tolerance tying B to A needed"]},{"id":"16.3","title":"Datum shift on a pilot with a bolt pattern","q":"A flange has primary face A and secondary pilot B, ⌀20.0 to 20.1 external, referenced at MMB. Four holes ⌀6.0 to 6.2 carry position ⌀0.2 Ⓜ | A | B Ⓜ. (a) What is B\u0027s MMB and simulator? (b) A produced pilot is 20.04 and a hole is 6.1. Find the greatest position error of that hole\u0027s axis from B (diametral), including bonus and shift. (c) Does the datum shift loosen the spacing between the four holes? (d) What distinguishes bonus from shift?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) External pilot, Rule #1: MMB = MMC = 20.1; a fixed ⌀20.1 gage bore, perpendicular to A.\n2. (b) Bonus = 6.1 − 6.0 = 0.1; shift = 20.1 − 20.04 = 0.06; total = 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.06 = 0.36 diametral.\n3. (c) No: shift moves the whole pattern as a group relative to B; hole-to-hole spacing stays within the tolerance.\n4. Bonus comes from the toleranced feature\u0027s (hole) departure from MMC; shift from the datum feature\u0027s (pilot) departure from MMB.","answers":["MMB 20.1","0.36 mm","Group shift only, spacing unchanged","Bonus from hole size, shift from datum size"]},{"id":"16.4","title":"Effect of datum order and modifier on the gage","q":"A part has a shaft ⌀10.0 to 10.2 (datum A) and a shoulder face (datum B). Three frames are proposed for the holes: (i) ⌀0.3 | A | B; (ii) ⌀0.3 | B | A; (iii) ⌀0.3 | B | A Ⓜ. For each, say which feature orients the part, how the A simulator behaves, and whether datum feature shift is permitted.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (i) A primary RMB: shaft held in an adjustable simulator, axis orients the part; B needs ≥1 contact point; holes oriented to axis A; no shift (RMB).\n2. (ii) B primary: face gets ≥3 points and orients the part; A RMB uses an adjustable simulator; holes orient to plane B; no shift.\n3. (iii) B primary orientation as in (ii), but A uses a fixed-size gage at MMB = 10.2, so datum feature shift is permitted.\n4. Changing order or modifier changes the relationship and the gage.","answers":["(i) shaft orients, RMB, no shift","(ii) face orients, A adjustable, no shift","(iii) face orients, A fixed at 10.2, shift allowed"]},{"id":"17.1","title":"Perpendicularity of a face to a base","q":"A block\u0027s side face carries a perpendicularity frame of 0.2 | A, where A is the base. (a) Describe the zone and the degrees of freedom involved. (b) With the base on a surface plate, an indicator swept over the whole side face reads a maximum of +0.14 and a minimum of −0.07 mm relative to a square. Does it pass? (c) What other form control is automatically limited? (d) How would the zone change with A | B in the frame?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Two parallel planes 0.2 apart, perpendicular to A, floating in location; only rotational DOF are controlled (3 DOF constrained by A).\n2. (b) Range = 0.14 − (−0.07) = 0.21 \u003e 0.2: fails.\n3. (c) Flatness of the face is also limited to 0.2 as every point lies within the zone.\n4. (d) The zone is perpendicular to A and also oriented to B (extra rotation), 5 DOF in all; it still does not locate the face.","answers":["Two planes 0.2 apart ⟂ A, orientation only","0.21 \u003e 0.2: fails","Flatness ≤ 0.2","B adds orientation about the A-normal"]},{"id":"17.2","title":"Perpendicularity at MMC on a hole","q":"A hole is dimensioned ⌀10.0 to 10.2 mm with perpendicularity ⌀0.1 Ⓜ | A. (a) What is the virtual condition and what gage checks it? (b) What is the total tolerance at 10.14 and at LMC? (c) A produced hole of 10.10 has its axis within a ⌀0.18 cylinder perpendicular to A. Does it pass? (d) Is the size tolerance still enforced?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Internal: VC = MMC − tol = 10.0 − 0.1 = 9.9; a fixed pin ⌀9.9 perpendicular to a datum face A, hole must accept it.\n2. (b) At 10.14: bonus 0.14, total 0.24. At LMC 10.2: bonus 0.2, total 0.3.\n3. (c) Allowed = 0.1 + 0.10 = 0.20 ≥ 0.18: passes.\n4. (d) Yes, size limits 10.0 to 10.2 are checked separately.","answers":["VC ⌀9.9, pin gage on datum face","0.24 at 10.14; 0.3 at LMC","Passes (0.18 ≤ 0.20)","Size still enforced"]},{"id":"17.3","title":"RFS or MMC perpendicularity on a slot","q":"A slot 12.0 to 12.2 wide has perpendicularity 0.1 | A under its size (RFS). A second design uses 0.1 Ⓜ | A. (a) What zone applies to the center plane in each case at a produced width of 12.15? (b) What is the VC in the second design? (c) Which would you choose for a slot that receives a loose-fitting tab, and which where a tight sliding guide fit matters? Why?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) RFS: two parallel planes 0.1 apart for every size, so 0.1 at 12.15. MMC: slot internal, MMC = 12.0; bonus = 12.15 − 12.0 = 0.15, total 0.25.\n2. (b) VC = 12.0 − 0.1 = 11.9.\n3. (c) MMC when function is assembly (loose tab): bonus gives free tolerance and a functional gage is possible, increasing acceptance. RFS when orientation must be kept tight regardless of size (guide fit).","answers":["RFS 0.1; MMC 0.25","VC 11.9","MMC for assembly, RFS for tight orientation"]},{"id":"17.4","title":"Find the errors in perpendicularity frames","q":"Five perpendicularity frames are proposed: (1) 0.1 Ⓜ | A on a planar surface; (2) 0.1 with no datum; (3) ⌀0.1 | A on a planar surface; (4) 0.1 Ⓣ | A on a hole\u0027s size dimension; (5) 0.2 | A on a hole already controlled by position ⌀0.1 | A | B | C. State what is wrong with each.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) Ⓜ is not allowed on a planar surface (no feature of size).\n2. (2) A datum reference is mandatory.\n3. (3) ⌀ not allowed on a planar surface.\n4. (4) Tangent plane applies only to surfaces; illegal on a feature of size.\n5. (5) Position with the same primary datum A already limits orientation at 0.1; a perpendicularity of 0.2 is larger and adds nothing: it must be a refinement, smaller than 0.1.","answers":["(1) M on surface illegal","(2) datum required","(3) ⌀ on surface illegal","(4) T on FOS illegal","(5) must be smaller than position\u0027s value"]},{"id":"18.1","title":"Parallelism of a top face with and without tangent plane","q":"A plate is 22.0 ±0.2 thick. Its top face has parallelism 0.1 | A, with A the bottom face. (a) Describe the zone and whether it also fixes the thickness. (b) An indicator sweep over the top face, relative to the bottom on a plate, has a maximum reading of 0.07 and a minimum of −0.05. Does it pass? (c) Suppose the frame were 0.1 Ⓣ | A and the plane touching the high points rises by 0.07 over the length while the low spots reach −0.05. Does it pass now? (d) What indirectly limits parallelism if no frame existed?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Two parallel planes 0.1 apart, parallel to A, floating anywhere between the size limits 21.8–22.2 (no location control). Also limits flatness.\n2. (b) Range 0.07 − (−0.05) = 0.12 \u003e 0.1: fails.\n3. (c) With tangent plane only the plane through the high points must lie in the zone: 0.07 ≤ 0.1, passes; low spots are not limited.\n4. (d) Rule #1 and the size limits: parallelism cannot exceed the size tolerance 0.4, and no datum means an inspector can choose either side.","answers":["Two planes 0.1 apart ∥ A, location free","0.12 \u003e 0.1: fails","With Ⓣ passes","Size limits/Rule #1 give ≤ 0.4"]},{"id":"18.2","title":"Hole parallelism at MMC","q":"A hole is dimensioned ⌀10.2 to 10.6 with parallelism ⌀0.1 Ⓜ | A (A a base plane). (a) State the virtual condition. (b) Give the allowed parallelism at 10.2, 10.4 and 10.6. (c) A hole at 10.35 has an axis tilted so it lies in a ⌀0.2 cylinder parallel to A. Does it pass? (d) Describe the functional gage.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Internal: VC = 10.2 − 0.1 = 10.1.\n2. (b) 0.1 at 10.2; 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3 at 10.4; 0.1 + 0.4 = 0.5 at 10.6 (LMC).\n3. (c) Allowed at 10.35 = 0.1 + 0.15 = 0.25 ≥ 0.2: passes.\n4. (d) A datum surface plus a ⌀10.1 pin parallel to it; the pin must enter the hole and float up and down because location is free.","answers":["VC ⌀10.1","0.1 / 0.3 / 0.5","Passes (0.2 ≤ 0.25)","⌀10.1 pin on a datum face, floating"]},{"id":"18.3","title":"Choosing parallelism for a linear guide","q":"A guide rail\u0027s top surface must remain parallel to its base within 0.05 to give smooth linear motion; the rail thickness is 30.0 ±0.1. The designer drafts: (1) 0.05 | A on the top surface; (2) 0.05 Ⓜ | A; (3) 0.05 with no datum; (4) 0.05 | A on a face drawn at 10° to A. Which are acceptable, and what is wrong with the rest?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (1) Acceptable: surface zone of two planes 0.05 apart parallel to A, 0.05 \u003c 0.2 size tolerance.\n2. (2) Illegal: Ⓜ cannot be used on a planar surface (not a feature of size).\n3. (3) No datum: does not comply, since parallelism needs a datum reference.\n4. (4) A face drawn at 10° is not nominally parallel; angularity would be the right control.","answers":["(1) acceptable","(2) M illegal on surface","(3) datum missing","(4) wrong control, use angularity"]},{"id":"18.4","title":"Redundant parallelism on a located hole","q":"A hole ⌀8.0 to 8.1 has position ⌀0.2 Ⓜ | A | B | C and a second frame parallelism ⌀0.3 | A. (a) Why does the parallelism add nothing? (b) What value would make it a real refinement, and what would it then control? (c) How much parallelism does the position frame indirectly permit at its MMC (8.0) and at LMC (8.1)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Position with the same primary datum A already orients the axis within ⌀0.2 (zone), so a larger parallelism 0.3 never limits anything.\n2. (b) A value below 0.2, e.g. ⌀0.1 | A; it then limits tilt of the axis relative to A more tightly than the location zone does.\n3. (c) Hole internal: MMC = 8.0; at 8.0 the position zone is 0.2 and at 8.1 (LMC) 0.3, so the tilt allowance is 0.2 at MMC and 0.3 at LMC.","answers":["Position already limits orientation to 0.2","Use \u003c 0.2","0.2 at MMC, 0.3 at LMC"]},{"id":"19.1","title":"Angularity of an inclined face and sine-bar check","q":"An inclined face of a block is shown at a basic 30° to datum plane A, with an angularity frame of 0.2 mm referencing A. (a) Describe the tolerance zone. (b) What does the callout not control? (c) The part is set on a sine bar at 30° to the surface plate (datum A on the plate) and an indicator is swept over the whole face: readings range from −0.14 to +0.12 mm. Does the face pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.2 apart, oriented at the basic 30° to datum A; the whole surface must lie between them.\n2. Not controlled: location (the zone floats in translation) and size; it does control form (flatness) of the face as a by-product.\n3. The sine bar sets the 30° face parallel to the plate, so full indicator movement = max − min = 0.12 − (−0.14) = 0.26 mm.\n4. 0.26 \u003e 0.2, so the face fails.","answers":["Two parallel planes 0.2 apart at basic 30° to A","Orientation and form only, not location","FIM = 0.26 mm \u003e 0.2: fails"]},{"id":"19.2","title":"Angularity on a tilted hole at MMC","q":"A hole of size ⌀8.0 to 8.2 mm is drilled at a basic 45° to datum plane A. Its frame reads angularity ⌀0.1 at MMC, A. (a) Find the virtual condition size and describe a functional gage. (b) What total angularity tolerance applies if the hole produces ⌀8.15 mm? (c) At ⌀8.15 mm the measured axis lies within a tilted cylinder of ⌀0.22 about the basic 45° line. Does it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Internal feature: MMC = smallest = 8.0. VC = MMC − tolerance = 8.0 − 0.1 = 7.9.\n2. Gage: fixed pin ⌀7.9 at the basic 45° to a plate representing A; the hole must accept the pin over its full length.\n3. Bonus at 8.15 = 8.15 − 8.0 = 0.15; total = 0.1 + 0.15 = 0.25 mm.\n4. 0.22 ≤ 0.25: passes (size limits are checked separately).","answers":["VC = 7.9 mm; fixed ⌀7.9 gage pin at 45° to A","Total zone = ⌀0.25 mm at 8.15","0.22 \u003c 0.25: passes"]},{"id":"19.3","title":"Spotting the error in a tapered-face callout","q":"A 50 mm long face is drawn at an angle of 60° ±0.5° to datum plane A. The designer also adds an angularity frame of 0.3 mm to A on the same face. (a) What is wrong? (b) Fix it. (c) Roughly what wedge does the ±0.5° allow over the 50 mm length, and what angularity value would be an equivalent replacement for that wedge?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Angularity needs a BASIC angle; a toleranced angle (±0.5°) combined with an angularity frame is contradictory and fails the CARE test.\n2. Fix: show 60° in a box (basic) and keep the angularity frame to A.\n3. Plus-minus wedge: 50 × tan 0.5° = 0.436 mm each side, about 0.87 mm total spread at the far end.\n4. Equivalent angularity ≈ 0.87 mm (a parallel-plane zone at basic 60°); the engineer should then set the value the function actually needs.","answers":["Toleranced angle with angularity is illegal; make the angle basic","About ±0.44 mm (0.87 total) at the far end","Equivalent angularity about 0.87 mm, then set by function"]},{"id":"19.4","title":"Angularity and position on the same angled hole","q":"An angled hole is located with position ⌀0.2 at MMC to datums A B C. The drafter also adds angularity ⌀0.3 at MMC to datum A on the same hole. (a) Is this acceptable? (b) What rule applies when an orientation tolerance and a location tolerance share the same datum reference frame order? (c) Suggest a legal value and why the angularity may still be wanted.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Position with basic angles already limits angularity indirectly (the axis must lie within the location zone).\n2. When angularity and position share the same datum reference, the angularity value must be less than the position value.\n3. 0.3 \u003e 0.2, so it is not acceptable. Use, say, ⌀0.1.\n4. A smaller angularity is justified when function needs tighter tilt than the location zone allows: the axis may still shift within the position zone but cannot tilt as much.","answers":["Not acceptable: 0.3 \u003e 0.2","Orientation tolerance must be smaller than the location tolerance","Use e.g. ⌀0.1 to tighten tilt within the position zone"]},{"id":"20.1","title":"Reading a position callout and its boundary","q":"A hole is dimensioned ⌀10.0 to 10.2 mm with the frame: position ⌀0.3 at MMC, datums A, B, C, with basic dimensions locating it. (a) Describe the tolerance zone at MMC. (b) What is the virtual condition? (c) What total position tolerance is allowed if the hole is ⌀10.15 mm? (d) At 10.1 mm, the axis lies 0.2 mm radially from true position. Does it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. At MMC (10.0) the zone is a cylinder ⌀0.3 centered on the true position, located relative to A, B, C.\n2. Internal feature: VC = MMC − tol = 10.0 − 0.3 = 9.7 mm (surface boundary).\n3. At 10.15 bonus = 0.15; total = 0.45.\n4. At 10.1 bonus = 0.1; total = 0.4; axis 0.2 radial = ⌀0.4 position: equal, passes.","answers":["Cylinder ⌀0.3 at MMC","VC = 9.7 mm","0.45 mm at 10.15","⌀0.4 actual = ⌀0.4 allowed: passes"]},{"id":"20.2","title":"Position from measured coordinates","q":"A hole is ⌀10.0 to 10.2 mm with position ⌀0.25 at MMC, A B C. True position is 40.00 mm from datum B and 25.00 mm from datum C. A CMM measures the hole axis at 40.12 mm and 24.90 mm, with the hole produced at ⌀10.10 mm. (a) Compute the position error. (b) Does it pass? (c) Would it pass if the hole were ⌀10.02 mm with the same axis position?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Δx = +0.12, Δy = −0.10.\n2. Position = 2√(Δx²+Δy²) = 2√(0.0144+0.0100) = 2(0.1562) = ⌀0.312.\n3. At 10.10: bonus 0.10; allowed 0.25 + 0.10 = 0.35 ≥ 0.312: passes.\n4. At 10.02: bonus 0.02; allowed 0.27 \u003c 0.312: fails.","answers":["Position error ⌀0.312 mm","Passes at 10.10 (allowed 0.35)","Fails at 10.02 (allowed 0.27)"]},{"id":"20.3","title":"Selecting the material condition modifier","q":"For each of the following, choose RFS, MMC or LMC for a position tolerance and give a one-line reason: (a) clearance holes for M6 bolts in a flange; (b) a bore whose axis must be held tightly regardless of its size because it locates a bearing; (c) a hole near the edge of a casting where minimum wall thickness must be guaranteed.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) MMC: pure assembly/clearance function, so extra hole size gives bonus tolerance without hurting fit; verified with a fixed functional gage.\n2. (b) RFS: axis location matters independent of size; no bonus allowed (default for features of size in 2009).\n3. (c) LMC: protects wall thickness; the tolerance grows as the hole departs from LMC while the boundary at LMC virtual condition preserves the wall.","answers":["(a) MMC, clearance fit","(b) RFS, no bonus","(c) LMC, minimum wall"]},{"id":"20.4","title":"Spot the error in a hole position callout","q":"A drawing locates a hole with coordinate dimensions 40 ±0.1 and 25 ±0.1 and adds a frame position ⌀0.2 at MMC with no datum references. Explain at least two problems and how to correct them.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Position requires basic (boxed) dimensions locating the true position; ± dimensions conflict with it (fails the CARE test).\n2. Position needs datum references to define where the zone is located; with none, the callout has no valid frame.\n3. Fix: replace the ± values with basic dimensions 40 and 25 and reference A B C (primary plane, then the two location datums).\n4. Result: a ⌀0.2 cylinder zone at MMC at the basic location, with bonus tolerance.","answers":["± dimensions must become basic","Datum references are missing","Corrected: position ⌀0.2 M A B C with basic 40 and 25"]},{"id":"21.1","title":"Position at RFS on a slot","q":"A slot is 6.0 to 6.2 mm wide, with position 0.2 RFS, datums A B; its center plane has a basic location 30 mm from B. Inspection finds the center plane 0.12 mm from true position, slot width 6.16. (a) Describe the zone. (b) Does it pass? (c) What would change if the frame were 0.2 at MMC?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.2 apart, centered on the basic center plane; the derived median plane must lie in it (±0.1 from true).\n2. RFS: no bonus. Deviation 0.12 \u003e 0.1: fails.\n3. At MMC (6.0) bonus = 6.16 − 6.0 = 0.16; total zone 0.36 (±0.18): 0.12 \u003c 0.18, passes.","answers":["Two planes 0.2 apart about the basic center plane","Fails: 0.12 offset \u003e 0.10","At MMC total 0.36: would pass"]},{"id":"21.2","title":"Four-hole pattern at MMC with a gage check","q":"Four holes, each ⌀6.0 to 6.2 mm, are in a pattern with position ⌀0.4 at MMC to A B (pattern dimensions basic). (a) Give the virtual condition and the fixed gage pin size. (b) Hole 1 is ⌀6.12 and its axis is 0.20 mm off true in x and 0.15 mm in y. Check it. (c) Hole 2 is ⌀6.02, offset 0.18 in x and 0.14 in y. Check it.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Internal feature: VC = 6.0 − 0.4 = 5.6; the gage has four fixed ⌀5.6 pins at the basic pattern.\n2. Hole 1: allowed = 0.4 + 0.12 = 0.52. Position = 2√(0.04+0.0225) = 2(0.25) = 0.50 ≤ 0.52: passes.\n3. Hole 2: allowed = 0.4 + 0.02 = 0.42; position = 2√(0.0324+0.0196) = 2(0.2280) = 0.456 \u003e 0.42: fails.","answers":["VC 5.6 mm; four fixed ⌀5.6 pins","Hole 1: 0.50 ≤ 0.52, passes","Hole 2: 0.456 \u003e 0.42, fails"]},{"id":"21.3","title":"Position at MMC on a coaxial stepped shaft","q":"A stepped shaft has a ⌀25.0 to 25.1 mm datum feature A and a ⌀12.0 to 12.1 mm journal with position ⌀0.06 at MMC to A (A applies at RFS). (a) What is the virtual condition of the journal, and the gage bore size? (b) At a journal size 12.04, what position tolerance is allowed? (c) The journal axis is 0.05 mm radially off the axis of A. Does it pass? (d) What is the maximum allowed at 12.0?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. External: VC = MMC + tol = 12.1 + 0.06 = 12.16; gage bore ⌀12.16 coaxial with the datum feature simulator.\n2. At 12.04: bonus = 12.10 − 12.04 = 0.06; allowed ⌀0.12.\n3. Offset 0.05 radial → ⌀0.10 ≤ 0.12: passes.\n4. At 12.0: bonus 0.10; allowed ⌀0.16.","answers":["VC 12.16 mm","⌀0.12 allowed at 12.04","⌀0.10 passes","⌀0.16 at 12.0"]},{"id":"21.4","title":"CMM check of a pin at RFS","q":"A pin is ⌀8.0 to 8.1 mm with position ⌀0.2 RFS to A B. A CMM measures the axis 0.07 mm off true in x and −0.09 mm in y, with the pin at ⌀8.00 mm. (a) Compute the position. (b) Does it pass? (c) What if the callout had instead been 0.2 at MMC?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Position = 2√(0.07² + 0.09²) = 2√(0.0049+0.0081) = 2(0.1140) = ⌀0.228.\n2. RFS: no bonus regardless of size; 0.228 \u003e 0.2: fails.\n3. At MMC: external MMC = 8.1; bonus at 8.00 = 0.1; allowed 0.3 ≥ 0.228: passes.","answers":["⌀0.228","Fails at RFS (no bonus)","Passes at MMC (allowed 0.3)"]},{"id":"22.1","title":"Why project the position zone of a threaded hole","q":"An M8 threaded hole in a base plate receives a bolt that clamps a 12 mm thick bracket on top. The hole has position ⌀0.3 to A B C with a projected tolerance zone of 12 mm height above the plate surface. (a) Why is the zone projected? (b) The hole axis passes through the true position at the plate surface but is tilted 1° from perpendicular. Does it pass with the projected zone? Without projection?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A tilted threaded hole can have its axis inside the zone within the plate but be displaced where the mating part sits; projection moves the zone out to the mating thickness, so the bolt position where the bracket\u0027s clearance hole sits is controlled.\n2. Offset at 12 mm: 12 × tan 1° = 0.2095 mm radial; the zone radius is 0.15, so with projection it fails.\n3. Without projection (zone inside the plate) the axis is at true position at the surface and within 0.15 along the thread, so it passes.","answers":["Controls the axis where the mating part sits","Projected: 0.21 \u003e 0.15, fails","Unprojected: passes"]},{"id":"22.2","title":"Composite position for a two-hole pattern","q":"Two holes, ⌀6.0 to 6.1 mm, are basically 40 mm apart. The composite frame has an upper segment ⌀0.5 at MMC to A B C and a lower segment ⌀0.2 at MMC to A. Both holes are ⌀6.05. Relative to the datum frame hole 1 is off true by (0.15, 0.10) mm and hole 2 by (0.21, 0.14) mm (x along the pair axis, y perpendicular). (a) What are the allowed values at ⌀6.05? (b) Check the upper segment. (c) Check the lower segment (the pattern may shift as a group; ignore rotation).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Bonus = 0.05; upper allowed 0.55, lower allowed 0.25.\n2. Upper: hole 1: 2√(0.0225+0.01) = 0.361; hole 2: 2√(0.0441+0.0196) = 0.505; both ≤ 0.55: passes.\n3. Lower: the pattern floats in location. Mean offset (0.18, 0.12); residuals hole 1 (−0.03, −0.02), hole 2 (+0.03, +0.02): position 2√(0.0009+0.0004) = 0.072 ≤ 0.25: passes.\n4. The lower segment refines spacing/orientation to A only.","answers":["Allowed 0.55 upper, 0.25 lower","Upper: 0.361 and 0.505, pass","Lower: 0.072 ≤ 0.25, passes"]},{"id":"22.3","title":"Zero position tolerance at MMC","q":"A hole is ⌀10.0 to 10.3 mm with position ⌀0 at MMC to A B C. (a) What is the virtual condition? (b) What position tolerance is allowed at ⌀10.12? (c) The axis is 0.05 mm radial from true at ⌀10.12. Does it pass? (d) What happens at ⌀10.0?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. VC = MMC − 0 = 10.0.\n2. Bonus = 10.12 − 10.0 = 0.12: allowed position ⌀0.12.\n3. Offset 0.05 radial = ⌀0.10 ≤ 0.12: passes.\n4. At 10.0 (MMC) nothing is allowed: the hole must be perfectly located. The tolerance comes entirely from size departure; the gage pin is ⌀10.0, the same as a conventional callout with VC 10.0.","answers":["VC = 10.0","⌀0.12 allowed at 10.12","Passes (⌀0.10)","At ⌀10.0 zero tolerance: perfect location"]},{"id":"22.4","title":"Position at LMC to protect a wall","q":"A hole ⌀20.0 to 20.2 mm is located 15.0 mm (basic) from a flat outer edge that is datum B. Its frame reads position ⌀0.3 at LMC, A B. (a) What is the LMC virtual condition? (b) What total position tolerance applies at ⌀20.1? (c) What minimum wall thickness between the hole and the edge is guaranteed (assume the edge is perfect)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. LMC (largest hole) = 20.2; LMC virtual condition = LMC + tol = 20.5 (internal feature).\n2. Bonus at 20.1 = 20.2 − 20.1 = 0.1; total 0.4.\n3. Worst wall = 15.0 − 20.5/2 = 15.0 − 10.25 = 4.75 mm.","answers":["VC(LMC) = 20.5 mm","0.4 mm at 20.1","Min wall = 4.75 mm"]},{"id":"23.1","title":"Position tolerances for a fixed-fastener joint","q":"An M8 bolt (max major diameter 8.0 mm) clamps two parts: one plate has threaded holes (projected zone), the other has clearance holes of ⌀8.4 to 8.8 mm. Both position tolerances are at MMC. (a) Find the position tolerance if both parts get equal values. (b) If the threaded part is given ⌀0.1, what does the clearance part get?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Fixed fastener: T = (H − F)/2 with H = minimum clearance hole 8.4 and F = 8.0.\n2. T = (8.4 − 8.0)/2 = 0.2 each (sum 0.4).\n3. If threaded = 0.1, clearance = 0.4 − 0.1 = 0.3.","answers":["T = ⌀0.2 mm each","Clearance part gets ⌀0.3"]},{"id":"23.2","title":"Floating fastener tolerances for two plates","q":"Two plates are bolted with M6 bolts (max 6.0 mm) passing through clearance holes in both plates; the minimum hole size is 6.6 mm in plate A and 7.0 mm in plate B. (a) Compute the position tolerance for plate A. (b) For plate B. (c) Compare with a fixed-fastener assembly using the same 6.6 mm holes.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Floating fastener: T = H − F for each plate.\n2. Plate A: 6.6 − 6.0 = ⌀0.6.\n3. Plate B: 7.0 − 6.0 = ⌀1.0.\n4. Fixed with 6.6 holes would be (6.6 − 6.0)/2 = ⌀0.3 each: floating allows twice as much.","answers":["⌀0.6 for plate A","⌀1.0 for plate B","Fixed would be ⌀0.3 (half)"]},{"id":"23.3","title":"Bonus tolerance consistency in a fixed joint","q":"In a fixed-fastener joint with an M8 bolt (max 8.0 mm), the threaded part has position ⌀0.2 (projected), and the clearance holes are ⌀8.4 to 8.8 with position ⌀0.2 at MMC. (a) If the clearance hole is actually ⌀8.65, what position tolerance applies? (b) Show that the bolt still fits at the worst offsets.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Bonus = 8.65 − 8.4 = 0.25; allowed 0.45.\n2. Offsets (radius): threaded hole 0.1, clearance hole 0.225, in opposite directions: centers 0.325 apart.\n3. Free play for an 8.0 bolt in an 8.65 hole: (8.65 − 8.0)/2 = 0.325, equal, so it just fits.","answers":["Allowed ⌀0.45","0.325 offset = 0.325 clearance: just fits"]},{"id":"23.4","title":"Checking a tolerance against the fastener formula","q":"An M6 bolt (max 6.0 mm) fastens into a threaded hole (position ⌀0.15, projected) through a clearance hole of minimum ⌀6.4 mm. The designer gives the clearance hole position ⌀0.3 at MMC. (a) Does it satisfy the fixed fastener formula? (b) What is the largest clearance hole tolerance? (c) What minimum hole size would make ⌀0.3 acceptable?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Fixed: T1 + T2 = H − F = 6.4 − 6.0 = 0.4.\n2. 0.15 + 0.3 = 0.45 \u003e 0.4: not acceptable.\n3. Max T2 = 0.4 − 0.15 = 0.25.\n4. For 0.45: H = 6.0 + 0.45 = 6.45 minimum hole.","answers":["Not acceptable (0.45 \u003e 0.4)","Max clearance-hole tolerance 0.25","H min = 6.45 mm"]},{"id":"24.1","title":"Circular runout of a shaft section","q":"A shaft with a ⌀30 mm middle section has two ⌀20 mm bearing journals at its ends forming datum axis A-B. The middle section has circular runout 0.05 mm to A-B. (a) Describe the zone and how the datum axis is established. (b) Indicator FIM at six sections: 0.03, 0.06, 0.04, 0.02, 0.05, 0.03 mm. Does the part pass? (c) Does the callout limit taper?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: at each cross-section, two concentric circles 0.05 apart (radial), centered on the datum axis; the surface circle must lie between them.\n2. Datum axis: the common axis of the two journals (V-blocks or bearing supports); the part is rotated about it.\n3. Each section FIM must be ≤ 0.05; section 2 = 0.06: fails.\n4. Circular runout is applied independently per section and does not limit taper.","answers":["Two concentric circles 0.05 apart at each section","Section 2 reads 0.06: fails","Taper not controlled"]},{"id":"24.2","title":"Total runout versus circular runout","q":"A ⌀30 mm cylindrical surface has total runout 0.07 mm to datum axis A. Indicator readings at each fixed section during rotation (FIM) are each below 0.04. With the indicator traversed along the full length while the part rotates, the full needle excursion is 0.08 mm. (a) Does it pass total runout? (b) Would it pass circular runout 0.05? (c) What does total runout control that circular does not?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two coaxial cylinders 0.07 apart radially about A.\n2. Total FIM = 0.08 \u003e 0.07: fails total runout.\n3. Circular runout 0.05: each section FIM \u003c 0.04 ≤ 0.05: passes.\n4. Total runout also controls taper and straightness/profile along the surface, in addition to circularity and coaxiality.","answers":["Fails total (0.08 \u003e 0.07)","Passes circular (\u003c 0.05)","Total also limits taper and straightness along the surface"]},{"id":"24.3","title":"Face runout on a flange","q":"A flange face has total runout 0.1 mm to datum axis A (the bore). The indicator moved radially across the face while the part rotates gives a 0.12 mm FIM. Circle-by-circle readings give at most 0.06 mm. (a) Describe the zone for total runout. (b) Pass or fail? (c) What if the callout were circular runout 0.1?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.1 apart, perpendicular to datum axis A.\n2. Total FIM 0.12 \u003e 0.1: fails.\n3. Circular runout 0.1: each circle 0.06 ≤ 0.1: passes.\n4. Total runout on a face controls perpendicularity and flatness together.","answers":["Two planes 0.1 apart perpendicular to A","Fails total (0.12)","Passes circular (0.06)"]},{"id":"24.4","title":"Which runout callouts are legal?","q":"Four proposed callouts: (a) circular runout 0.05 at MMC to A; (b) total runout 0.1 to a flat datum plane A; (c) circular runout 0.05 to A-B (two journals); (d) a runout frame under the size dimension of a hole. Which are legal under ASME Y14.5-2009? Fix the others.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Illegal: runout is RFS only; no material modifiers.\n2. (b) Illegal: runout requires a datum axis; use an axis such as A from a cylinder.\n3. (c) Legal: a common datum axis from two journals.\n4. (d) Illegal: runout applies to surfaces, not to the axis of a size feature; use position (or runout on the surface).","answers":["(c) is the only legal callout","(a) no modifiers","(b) needs a datum axis","(d) surfaces only"]},{"id":"25.1","title":"Concentricity from opposed point readings","q":"A ⌀30 mm collar has concentricity ⌀0.1 to datum axis A. In one cross-section, two diametrically opposed surface points are measured relative to A as (15.06, 0.04) and (−14.98, −0.02) mm. (a) Describe the zone. (b) Compute the median point and check conformance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: cylinder ⌀0.1 centered on datum axis A, in which all median points of diametrically opposed elements must lie (RFS).\n2. Median = ((15.06−14.98)/2, (0.04−0.02)/2) = (0.04, 0.01).\n3. Distance = √(0.0016+0.0001) = 0.0412; diametral = 0.0825 ≤ 0.1: passes.","answers":["Cylinder ⌀0.1 on A for median points","Median (0.04, 0.01); ⌀0.0825","Passes"]},{"id":"25.2","title":"Symmetry versus position for a slot","q":"A slot has symmetry 0.2 mm to a datum center plane A. Measured wall locations relative to A are −4.96 mm and +5.19 mm. (a) Describe the zone. (b) Does it pass? (c) If it were instead position 0.2 at MMC (slot 10.0 to 10.3), would it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.2 apart symmetric about A for the median points of opposed elements, RFS.\n2. Median offset = (−4.96 + 5.19)/2 = 0.115 \u003e 0.1: fails.\n3. Width 10.15; bonus = 10.15 − 10.0 = 0.15; allowed 0.35 total (±0.175); 0.115 within: passes.","answers":["Two planes 0.2 apart about A","Fails (0.115 \u003e 0.1)","Passes position at MMC"]},{"id":"25.3","title":"Choosing runout, concentricity or position","q":"Choose a control for each need and explain briefly: (a) a belt pulley groove must not wobble relative to the bore; (b) a high-speed rotor must be dynamically balanced with its mass centered; (c) a pin on a shaft must fit a mating hole with clearance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Runout: easy to inspect, controls both circularity and coaxiality of the surface.\n2. (b) Concentricity: controls median points, hence mass centering for balance; costly, so only when needed.\n3. (c) Position at MMC: assembly fit gets bonus tolerance and a fixed gage can be used.","answers":["(a) circular runout","(b) concentricity","(c) position at MMC"]},{"id":"25.4","title":"Which concentricity and symmetry callouts are legal?","q":"Callouts: (a) concentricity ⌀0.1 at MMC to A; (b) concentricity ⌀0.1 to A under a cylinder\u0027s diameter dimension; (c) symmetry 0.2 to A under a slot width; (d) symmetry with a diameter symbol, ⌀0.2 to A. Which are legal in ASME Y14.5-2009?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Illegal: concentricity is RFS only; no modifier.\n2. (b) Legal: applies to a feature of revolution to a datum axis.\n3. (c) Legal: planar median points of a width.\n4. (d) Illegal: symmetry has a two-plane zone, no diameter symbol.","answers":["(b) and (c) legal","(a) no modifiers","(d) no diameter symbol"]},{"id":"26.1","title":"Interpreting a profile of a surface zone","q":"A curved surface has profile 0.4 mm to A B C with all dimensions basic. Deviations from the true profile at four points: +0.15, −0.18, +0.22, −0.05 mm (+ is outside the material). (a) Describe the zone. (b) Does it pass? (c) If the drawing instead showed a unilateral zone 0.4 wide entirely outside the true profile, would it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: bilateral, two boundaries 0.2 either side of the true profile, fixed to the datum frame.\n2. +0.22 \u003e +0.2: fails.\n3. Unilateral outward: zone from 0 to +0.4: −0.18 lies outside it: fails (+0.22 would be fine).","answers":["Band ±0.2 about the true profile","Fails (+0.22)","Fails unilateral (−0.18)"]},{"id":"26.2","title":"Profile of a line or of a surface","q":"For a turbine blade, decide the control: (a) the airfoil sections must follow the profile but twist along the blade is acceptable; (b) the entire airfoil surface must be held in 3D. Name the tolerance and the zone in each case.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Profile of a line, section by section; the zone is a 2D band in each cut and there is no control between sections, so twist is permitted.\n2. (b) Profile of a surface; a 3D zone bounded by two surfaces offset from the true profile.","answers":["(a) profile of a line","(b) profile of a surface"]},{"id":"26.3","title":"Profile with and without datum references","q":"A curved surface has deviations from the true shape at four points of 0.10, 0.22, 0.14, 0.05 mm. Compare: (a) profile 0.2 with no datums; (b) profile 0.2 to A B C. Which pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) No datum: the zone floats and controls form/size only: range = 0.22 − 0.05 = 0.17 ≤ 0.2: passes.\n2. (b) Fixed to the datum frame: ±0.1; 0.22 exceeds: fails.\n3. Datum references lock the zone in location and orientation.","answers":["(a) passes, range 0.17","(b) fails, 0.22 \u003e 0.1"]},{"id":"26.4","title":"All-around profile of a pocket outline","q":"A pocket outline has profile 0.3 to A B with the all-around symbol. (a) Which surfaces are covered? (b) What is the zone? (c) A wall measures 0.17 mm from true. Pass or fail?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. All around covers every surface on the outline of the pocket (the walls), not the floor.\n2. Zone: ±0.15 about the true profile, fixed to A B.\n3. 0.17 \u003e 0.15: fails.","answers":["Walls only","±0.15 band","Fails"]},{"id":"27.1","title":"Locating a planar wall with profile","q":"A wall is located basic 30 mm from datum B with profile 0.2 mm to A B. Measured distances from B at four points: 30.06, 30.13, 29.95, 30.05 mm. (a) Describe the zone. (b) Does it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone: two parallel planes 0.2 apart, centered at 30 from B, fixed (±0.1).\n2. 30.13 is +0.13 \u003e 0.1: fails.\n3. The zone also controls flatness and orientation of the wall.","answers":["Planes 0.2 apart centered at 30 from B","Fails (+0.13)"]},{"id":"27.2","title":"Coplanar pads with profile","q":"Two pads are called coplanar with profile 0.2 mm to A (basic 10 from A). Heights above A: 10.04, 10.09, 9.93, 9.89 mm. (a) Pass or fail? (b) If the callout had no datum (coplanarity only), would it pass?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Located zone: 9.9 to 10.1; 9.89 lies outside: fails.\n2. Floating zone: range 10.09 − 9.89 = 0.20 ≤ 0.2: just passes.","answers":["Fails (9.89 \u003c 9.9)","Floating: passes at 0.20"]},{"id":"27.3","title":"Composite versus single-segment profile","q":"A composite profile has an upper segment 1.0 mm to A B and a lower segment 0.3 mm to A. Measured deviations from the true profile in the datum frame: Case 1: +0.35, +0.30, +0.28, +0.32. Case 2: −0.12, +0.30, +0.05, +0.20. Evaluate both cases.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Upper: ±0.5; both cases are within.\n2. Lower floats in location relative to A (orientation only): Case 1 range 0.07 ≤ 0.3 passes; Case 2 range 0.42 \u003e 0.3 fails.","answers":["Case 1 passes","Case 2 fails the lower segment"]},{"id":"27.4","title":"Profile of a cone","q":"A conical surface (half-angle 20° between surface and axis) has profile 0.08 mm to datum axis A. (a) Describe the zone. (b) What maximum pure radial deviation is allowed? (c) What maximum pure axial shift is allowed?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Zone ±0.04 normal to the surface.\n2. Radial: 0.04/cos 20° = 0.0426 mm.\n3. Axial: 0.04/sin 20° = 0.117 mm.","answers":["±0.04 normal","0.0426 mm radial","0.117 mm axial"]}];
